        {
          "id": "pthm-emx-uniaxial-dispersion",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Ordinary and extraordinary sheets",
          "statement": "In the homogeneous lossless plane-wave model, let $\\varepsilon=\\operatorname{diag}(\\varepsilon_\\perp,\\varepsilon_\\perp,\\varepsilon_\\parallel)$ with $\\varepsilon_\\perp,\\varepsilon_\\parallel>0$ in $\\mathrm{F\\,m^{-1}}$, let $\\mu>0$ be a scalar in $\\mathrm{H\\,m^{-1}}$, and let $\\omega>0$ be in $\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. For a nonzero source-free mode with real wavevector $k=(k_x,0,k_z)\\ne0$ in $\\mathrm{m^{-1}}$, the nonzero-mode wavevectors lie on exactly the following two sheets:\n\n$$k_x^2+k_z^2=\\mu\\varepsilon_\\perp\\omega^2,\\qquad \\frac{k_x^2}{\\varepsilon_\\parallel}+\\frac{k_z^2}{\\varepsilon_\\perp}=\\mu\\omega^2.$$\n\nAway from an intersection the first sheet is the ordinary sector, whose electric amplitude is parallel to $\\hat y$, and the second is the extraordinary sector, whose electric amplitude lies in the $xz$ plane; each then has a one-dimensional polarization space. At an intersection the two sectors span a two-dimensional polarization space. If $\\varepsilon_\\perp=\\varepsilon_\\parallel=\\varepsilon$, the isotropic specialization also allows arbitrary real $k\\ne0$, without the $xz$-plane restriction: its nonzero-mode wavevectors lie in every direction on the common sphere $|k|^2=\\mu\\varepsilon\\omega^2$, and at every such wavevector the polarization space is the two-dimensional complex subspace $\\{e\\in\\mathbb C^3:k\\cdot e=0\\}$. In the $xz$ section the ordinary/extraordinary labels then denote the two coordinate sectors, not distinct dispersion branches. If $\\varepsilon_\\perp\\ne\\varepsilon_\\parallel$, the sheets intersect only for $k_x=0$ and $k_z^2=\\mu\\varepsilon_\\perp\\omega^2$; at those optic-axis directions the polarization space is again two-dimensional. The zero amplitude is not a mode in this statement.\n\nThe symbol classification holds algebraically for every $\\omega>0$. For physical interpretation, declare a finite band $I_\\omega=[\\omega_-,\\omega_+]$ with $0<\\omega_-<\\omega_+<\\infty$, in $\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, and use the constant-coefficient model only for $\\omega\\in I_\\omega$, as in its defining material closure.",
