        {
          "id": "pthm-emx-lorentz-reciprocity",
          "kind": "physics-theorem",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Reciprocity in reciprocal linear media",
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded domain with $C^1$ boundary and outward unit normal $n$. Fix a real angular frequency $\\omega$ in $\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Let $E_j,H_j\\in C^1(\\overline\\Omega;\\mathbb C^3)$ and $J_j\\in C^0(\\overline\\Omega;\\mathbb C^3)$, for $j=1,2$, be phasor fields and electric-current densities in $\\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$, $\\mathrm{A\\,m^{-1}}$, and $\\mathrm{A\\,m^{-2}}$, respectively. Suppose both pairs solve the fixed-frequency Maxwell equations there with the same continuous coefficient maps $\\varepsilon,\\mu:\\overline\\Omega\\to\\mathbb C^{3\\times3}$ and $\\varepsilon(x)^T=\\varepsilon(x)$, $\\mu(x)^T=\\mu(x)$ at every $x$. Use Euclidean volume measure $dV$ and boundary area measure $dS$. Then\n\n$$\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}n\\cdot(E_1\\times H_2-E_2\\times H_1)\\,dS=\\int_\\Omega(E_2\\cdot J_1-E_1\\cdot J_2)\\,dV.$$\n\nThe dot and cross products in this identity are the complex-bilinear products defined in the reciprocal phasor model; no complex conjugation occurs. Both integrals have units W. In particular, the real symmetric positive permittivity tensor and positive scalar permeability of the lossless anisotropic plane-wave model satisfy these reciprocal-coefficient hypotheses, so the algebraic identity applies to any two such phasor solutions restricted to a bounded $C^1$ subdomain. Physical use of that anisotropic branch is restricted to its declared finite positive-frequency band $I_\\omega=[\\omega_-,\\omega_+]$, $0<\\omega_-<\\omega_+<\\infty$; the reciprocity identity itself asserts no broadband material response.",
