        {
          "id": "ex-emx-extraordinary-longitudinal-e",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Electric amplitude has a longitudinal component",
          "statement": "Let $\\varepsilon=\\operatorname{diag}(\\varepsilon_\\perp,\\varepsilon_\\perp,\\varepsilon_\\parallel)$ with $\\varepsilon_\\perp,\\varepsilon_\\parallel>0$ and $\\varepsilon_\\perp\\ne\\varepsilon_\\parallel$, and let $\\mu>0$. Fix $\\omega>0$ and real $k=(k_x,0,k_z)$ with $k_xk_z\\ne0$ on the extraordinary sheet $k_x^2/\\varepsilon_\\parallel+k_z^2/\\varepsilon_\\perp=\\mu\\omega^2$. Choose $d_0\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$ in $\\mathrm{C\\,m^{-1}}$ and set the displacement amplitude $d=d_0(k_z,0,-k_x)$ and electric amplitude $e=\\varepsilon^{-1}d$. Then $k\\cdot d=0$ but $k\\cdot e=d_0k_xk_z(1/\\varepsilon_\\perp-1/\\varepsilon_\\parallel)\\ne0$. Thus the extraordinary electric amplitude is not orthogonal to the wavevector even though the displacement amplitude is.\n\nThe algebraic example holds for every $\\omega>0$. For physical interpretation, declare a finite angular-frequency band $I_\\omega=[\\omega_-,\\omega_+]$ with $0<\\omega_-<\\omega_+<\\infty$, in $\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, and use the constant-coefficient model only for $\\omega\\in I_\\omega$, as in the supplying uniaxial classification.",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-emx-uniaxial-dispersion"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "pthm-emx-uniaxial-dispersion": "physical-result"
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Jin Au Kong, Electromagnetic Wave Theory, last edition 2008, PIERS free release 2021",
                "url": "https://piers.org/piers/files/BOOK_ElectromagneticWaveTheory_by_JINAUKONG_FreeRelease2021.pdf",
                "locator": "Chapter 3, §3.3.E, printed pp. 317–318, equations (3.3.68)–(3.3.71): the extraordinary displacement is perpendicular to k while its electric amplitude lies in the optic-axis plane and is generally not parallel to D."
              }
            ]
          },
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "physical_scope": "A conditional bulk plane-wave example in a homogeneous lossless uniaxial dielectric with positive principal permittivities, positive scalar permeability, a real nonzero wavevector on the extraordinary sheet, and nonzero transverse and axial wavevector components. Physical interpretation is restricted to a declared finite positive-frequency band of effective phase response; the algebraic example holds for every positive frequency. It makes no interface, finite-beam or empirical claim.",
          "strategy": "direct"
        },
        {
          "id": "ex-emx-reciprocity-not-time-reversal",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Loss and reciprocity coexist",
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Choose a one-oscillator Lorentz response with $\\varepsilon_\\infty>0$ in $\\mathrm{F\\,m^{-1}}$, $a>0$ in $\\mathrm{F\\,m^{-1}\\,s^{-2}}$, $\\omega_0>0$ and $\\gamma>0$ in $\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, and fix $\\omega>0$ in $\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. In the $e^{-i\\omega t}$ convention its scalar absolute permittivity is $\\varepsilon(\\omega)=\\varepsilon_\\infty+a/(\\omega_0^2-\\omega^2-i\\gamma\\omega)$. Let $I_3$ denote the $3\\times3$ identity matrix, and let the tensor coefficients be $\\boldsymbol\\varepsilon(\\omega)=\\varepsilon(\\omega)I_3$ and $\\boldsymbol\\mu=\\mu I_3$ with real $\\mu>0$ in $\\mathrm{H\\,m^{-1}}$. These coefficients satisfy the transpose-symmetry condition of the reciprocal phasor model, while $\\operatorname{Im}\\varepsilon(\\omega)>0$. For any nonzero local electric amplitude $e\\in\\mathbb C^3$ in $\\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$, define the real electric field at a fixed material point by $E(t)=\\operatorname{Re}(e e^{-i\\omega t})$. Let $p_1=(\\varepsilon(\\omega)-\\varepsilon_\\infty)e$ be the oscillator polarization amplitude from the one-oscillator response, in $\\mathrm{C\\,m^{-2}}$; put $P_1(t)=\\operatorname{Re}(p_1e^{-i\\omega t})$, and define its polarization-current contribution by $J_{\\mathrm{osc}}(t):=\\partial_tP_1(t)$. Its phasor amplitude is $j_{\\mathrm{osc}}=-i\\omega(\\varepsilon(\\omega)-\\varepsilon_\\infty)e$ in $\\mathrm{A\\,m^{-2}}$. The cycle-averaged power density transferred from the electric field to the oscillator through this current contribution, in $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-3}}$, is\n\n$$\\overline q_{\\mathrm{osc}}:=\\frac1T\\int_0^T J_{\\mathrm{osc}}(t)\\cdot E(t)\\,dt=\\frac{\\omega}{2}\\operatorname{Im}\\varepsilon(\\omega)|e|^2>0,\\qquad T=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega},\\qquad |e|^2:=e^*\\cdot e=\\sum_{r=1}^3|e_r|^2.$$\n\nHere $e^*$ is componentwise complex conjugation. The conjugate used in this cycle average is distinct from the bilinear, unconjugated product in the reciprocity identity.\n\nThus the coefficient condition that supplies the Lorentz reciprocity identity is compatible with positive cycle-averaged work on the oscillator-current contribution in this adopted model.\n\n",
          "deps": [
            "pthm-emx-lorentz-reciprocity",
            "pthm-emx-lorentz-susceptibility",
            "def-emx-reciprocal-phasor-medium",
            "def-countable-choice"
