          "statement": "Let Omega=(0,a) x (0,b) x (0,d) with a,b,d>0 metres, set e_z:=(0,0,1), and take constant scalar epsilon>0 in F m^-1 and mu>0 in H m^-1, with zero conductivity and zero forcing. For integers m,n>=1 define\n\n$$f_{mn}(x,y):=\\sin\\frac{m\\pi x}{a}\\sin\\frac{n\\pi y}{b},\\qquad k_{mn}^2:=\\left(\\frac{m\\pi}{a}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{n\\pi}{b}\\right)^2,\\qquad k_{mn}:=\\sqrt{k_{mn}^2}>0,\\qquad \\omega_{mn}:=\\frac{k_{mn}}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon\\mu}}>0.$$\n\nHere $k_{mn}$ is the positive transverse wavenumber in $\\mathrm{m}^{-1}$, $\\omega_{mn}$ is the angular frequency in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, and $t\\in\\mathbb R$ in the time-dependent field below is SI time in seconds. The profile $f_{mn}$ is dimensionless.\n\nFix E_*>0 in V m^-1 and set u(x,y,z):=E_* f_{mn}(x,y)e_z, the spatial electric amplitude. This is the beta=0 TM member of the already verified rectangular mode family; its real time-dependent electric field is u cos(omega_mn t), with the associated magnetic field given by that mode. It satisfies the tangential-electric PEC condition on the four side walls because f_mn vanishes there, and on the end walls because u is parallel to their normal e_z. The TM mode has B_z=0, so the normal-magnetic PEC condition also holds on the end walls; the mode supplier gives that condition on the side walls. Define V:=closure(C_c^\\infty(Omega;R3)) in the real H(curl;Omega) graph norm and, for w,v in V, define the real-frequency form\n\n$$a_{\\omega_{mn}}(w,v):=\\int_\\Omega\\left(\\mu^{-1}\\operatorname{curl}w\\cdot\\operatorname{curl}v-\\omega_{mn}^2\\varepsilon w\\cdot v\\right)\\,dx.$$\n\nThis is the weak operator obtained by replacing +s^2 epsilon in the positive-shift problem by -omega_mn^2 epsilon. It has a nonzero kernel at omega_mn.",
          "strategy": "direct"
        }
