          },
          "physical_scope": "Conditional result for an ideal scalar two-beam classical phasor model with a common ensemble, finite second moments, a controllable full relative-phase sweep, and mean-square amplitude as the detector-response model. The $\\Gamma_{12}$ here is the cross-moment between two beam channels; it is not the transverse-polarization cross-component of one beam. The visibility is the model contrast, not a finite-sample estimate. No detector calibration, noise model, empirical observation, or unconditional claim about measured fringe visibility is made.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $A_1,A_2:\\Xi\\to\\mathbb C$ be jointly measurable classical phasor amplitudes\nat one fixed observation point for a single selected scalar field component,\non one probability space, each in V m$^{-1}$, with\n$\\mathbb E(|A_1|^2+|A_2|^2)<\\infty$. Define\n$I_j:=\\mathbb E[|A_j|^2]$ and\n$\\Gamma_{12}:=\\mathbb E[A_1\\overline{A_2}]$. Adopt the ideal scalar\ntwo-beam model with a phase multiplier ranging over the full unit circle\n$\\mathbb T:=\\{\\zeta\\in\\mathbb C:|\\zeta|=1\\}$. For each $\\zeta\\in\\mathbb T$,\nset $A_\\zeta:=A_2+\\zeta A_1$ and $I(\\zeta):=\\mathbb E[|A_\\zeta|^2]$.\nThe quantities $I_j$ and $I(\\zeta)$\nare mean-square field coordinates in (V m$^{-1}$)$^2$, not calibrated\nirradiances. Set\n$I_{\\max}:=\\max_{\\zeta\\in\\mathbb T}I(\\zeta)$ and\n$I_{\\min}:=\\min_{\\zeta\\in\\mathbb T}I(\\zeta)$. When $I_1+I_2>0$, define the\nmodel visibility by\n$$V:=\\frac{I_{\\max}-I_{\\min}}{I_{\\max}+I_{\\min}}.$$\nThen\n$$I(\\zeta)=I_1+I_2+2\\operatorname{Re}(\\zeta\\Gamma_{12}),\\qquad V=\\frac{2|\\Gamma_{12}|}{I_1+I_2}\\le1.$$",
