          "id": "ex-emx-single-port-lossless-reflection",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A closed lossless one-port has unit reflection modulus",
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $\\mathcal I\\subset(0,\\infty)$ be a set of admissible angular frequencies at which a closed lossless one-channel finite port model is defined, with no unlisted outgoing power. At each $\\omega\\in\\mathcal I$, assume the solution satisfies all hypotheses of [[pthm-emx-scattering-contraction]]: the source-free macroscopic Maxwell equations with $\\rho_f=J_f=0$, a smooth $T$-periodic real solution and material states on one of the stated homogeneous finite Lorentz or Drude branches, and the stated passive-wall and outgoing-power bookkeeping. Here $T=2\\pi/\\omega$; material, wall, and unlisted-output losses vanish for every input. Write the scalar scattering matrix at each frequency as $S(\\omega)=[r(\\omega)]$. Then $|r(\\omega)|=1$ for each such frequency. This fixes reflected power but leaves the phase unconstrained by power conservation: any unit-modulus scalar has the same power factor. For any real-valued function $\\phi:\\mathcal I\\to\\mathbb R$, $r_\\phi(\\omega)=e^{i\\phi(\\omega)}$ has unit modulus; when this circle-valued function is nonconstant, its phase varies. If $\\mathcal I$ contains two distinct frequencies, such a varying family exists: set $\\phi$ equal to $0$ and $\\pi$ at those two frequencies, respectively, and set $\\phi=0$ elsewhere. This is an algebraic freedom allowed by the power constraint, not a claim that every such phase law is realized by a Maxwell device.\n\n",
