          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "physics",
          "title": "A visible subblock need not be unitary",
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$.\n\nFor $z,w\\in\\mathbb C^2$, use the Hermitian inner product $\\langle z,w\\rangle=\\overline z_1w_1+\\overline z_2w_2$ and norm $\\|z\\|_2^2=\\langle z,z\\rangle$. For $A\\in\\mathbb C^{2\\times2}$, let $A^\\dagger=\\overline A^{\\,\\mathsf T}$, with componentwise conjugation and ordinary transpose; $A$ is unitary when $A^\\dagger A=AA^\\dagger=I_2$, where $I_2$ is the identity on $\\mathbb C^2$.\n\nConsider the algebraic two-channel scattering matrix\n\n$$S=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}.$$\n\nThe displayed matrix is unitary. Let $e_1=(1,0)^T$ and $e_2=(0,1)^T$. A one-channel observer retains only channel 1, represented by $P=\\operatorname{diag}(1,0)$, which satisfies $P^2=P=P^\\dagger$ and is the orthogonal projection onto $\\operatorname{span}\\{e_1\\}$. Identify its range $\\operatorname{ran}P=\\operatorname{span}\\{e_1\\}$ with $\\mathbb C$ using the basis vector $e_1$. For incident vector $a=Ae_1$, where $|A|^2=1\\,\\mathrm W$, the full output is $Sa=Ae_2$, while the observed output is $PSa=0$. The observed operator $S_{\\mathrm{obs}}:=PS|_{\\operatorname{ran}P}:\\operatorname{ran}P\\to\\operatorname{ran}P$ is represented by the one-by-one matrix $[0]$, so it is not unitary even though the full two-channel map conserves power. The omitted second output accounts for the power; there is no material absorption in this example. The matrix is a consistency example, not a claim that a specified waveguide device realizes it.\n\n",
