        },
        {
          "id": "lem-emg-fx-conformal-constraints",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Exact conformal electrovacuum constraint transformation",
          "reference": "items/lem-emg-fx-conformal-constraints.md#statement",
          "design_state": "Derives the exact divergence, norm, trace, curvature, Hamiltonian, and retained momentum transformations from the stated seed and SI constraints.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": true,
          "physical_scope": null,
          "statement": "Use the smooth conformal seed data defined in this development on an oriented smooth three-manifold, and let $D$ and $\\bar D$ be the Levi-Civita connections of $h=\\psi^4\\bar h$ and $\\bar h$. Define $\\Delta_{\\bar h}\\psi:=\\bar h^{ij}\\bar D_i\\partial_j\\psi$, with the Euclidean convention $\\Delta=\\sum_i\\partial_i^2$ in Cartesian coordinates. Use the spatial curvature sign and scalar-curvature contraction fixed for induced data in the preceding Gauss-Codazzi convention. Let $e=\\alpha^{-1}\\psi^{-6}\\bar E$ and $b=\\alpha^{-1}\\psi^{-6}\\bar B$ be the SI-convention vector fields associated with the inherited constrained electrovacuum initial-data convention. Then the two Maxwell divergence constraints are equivalent to\n\n$$\\bar D_i\\bar E^i=0,\\qquad \\bar D_i\\bar B^i=0,$$\n\nand the SI Hamiltonian constraint in that definition is equivalent to\n\n$$-8\\Delta_{\\bar h}\\psi+\\bar R\\,\\psi-|\\bar A|_{\\bar h}^2\\psi^{-7}+\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\tau^2-2\\Lambda\\right)\\psi^5-2\\left(|\\bar E|_{\\bar h}^2+|\\bar B|_{\\bar h}^2\\right)\\psi^{-3}=0.$$\n\nHere $\\bar R$ is the scalar curvature of $\\bar h$ in the stated convention, and the barred norms use $\\bar h$ to raise and lower indices. The exact vector divergence identities are $D_iE^i=\\psi^{-6}\\bar D_i\\bar E^i$ and $D_iB^i=\\psi^{-6}\\bar D_i\\bar B^i$. The separate momentum constraint remains\n\n$$D^jK_{ji}-D_i\\tau=-2\\psi^{-6}(\\bar E\\times_{\\bar h}\\bar B)_i,$$\n\nwhere the cross product on the right is lowered with $\\bar h$. In particular, if $K=0$ and $\\bar E\\times_{\\bar h}\\bar B=0$, the momentum equation holds. The transformed scalar equation alone does not impose the momentum constraint and gives no elliptic existence theorem.",
          "strategy": "direct",
          "deps": [
            "def-emg-fx-conformal-seed",
