          "physical_scope": null,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Fix nonnegative integers $n_0,n_1,n_2,n_3$ and ordered coordinate bases, and put $C^k:=\\mathbb R^{n_k}$ for $k=0,1,2,3$. The dual space $(C^k)^*$ is the vector space of linear functionals $C^k\\to\\mathbb R$, with the dual basis to the chosen coordinate basis. Let $D_0\\in\\mathbb R^{n_1\\times n_0}$, $D_1\\in\\mathbb R^{n_2\\times n_1}$, and $D_2\\in\\mathbb R^{n_3\\times n_2}$ be linear maps satisfying $D_1D_0=0$ and $D_2D_1=0$. This data is a finite cochain complex. The transpose $D_k^{\\mathsf T}$ denotes the representing matrix of the dual linear map in the dual bases. Let $M_1\\in\\mathbb R^{n_1\\times n_1}$ and $M_2\\in\\mathbb R^{n_2\\times n_2}$ be symmetric positive-definite matrices: $v^{\\mathsf T}M_kv>0$ for each nonzero $v\\in C^k$. They represent linear maps $M_1:C^1\\to(C^1)^*$ and $M_2:C^2\\to(C^2)^*$ in the chosen bases; in positive dimensions their inverses exist by the finite-dimensional positive-matrix result cited below, and the unique map on a zero-dimensional space is its own inverse.\n\nThe finite state space is $\\mathcal X:=C^2\\oplus(C^1)^*$, with state $(b,d)$, where $b$ is a column of $C^2$ coordinates and $d$ is a column in the dual basis of $(C^1)^*$. In this item, “phase space” means this finite-dimensional state space only; no symplectic form is asserted. On an open interval $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$, a semidiscrete trajectory is a continuously differentiable map $t\\mapsto(b(t),d(t))\\in\\mathcal X$ satisfying\n\n$$\\dot b(t)=-D_1M_1^{-1}d(t),\\qquad \\dot d(t)=D_1^{\\mathsf T}M_2b(t).$$\n\nIts two discrete constraints are $D_2b(t)=0$ in $C^3$ and $D_0^{\\mathsf T}d(t)=0$ in $(C^0)^*$. The associated quadratic energy is\n\n$$\\mathcal E(b,d):=\\tfrac12\\left(b^{\\mathsf T}M_2b+d^{\\mathsf T}M_1^{-1}d\\right).$$\n\nAll objects here are finite-dimensional real linear-algebra data. The term “Maxwell” names this algebraic model and does not identify it with a continuum Maxwell or Einstein–Maxwell solution.",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Ralf Hiptmair, Maxwell’s Equations: Continuous and Discrete, ETH Research Report 2015-18",
                "url": "https://www.sam.math.ethz.ch/sam_reports/reports_final/reports2015/2015-18.pdf",
                "locator": "Complete 56-page report inspected. §§3.1–3.3, printed pp. 23–26 (PDF pp. 24–27): finite cochains, signed incidence matrices, D_{k+1}D_k=0, and the separate scope of cochain sampling."
