        {
          "id": "alg-emg-fx-leapfrog-cochains",
          "kind": "algorithm",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Finite compatible leapfrog evolution",
          "reference": "research/plan-einstein-maxwell-models-frontier-expansion-track.md#emg-fx10-compatible-discrete-maxwell-constraints-and-finite-algorithms",
          "design_state": "Step 1 authored draft; readiness recorded separately.",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-emg-fx-cochain-maxwell"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-emg-fx-cochain-maxwell": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "physical_scope": null,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Use the finite cochain data fixed by the preceding definition item, namely finite real coordinate spaces $C^0,C^1,C^2,C^3$, maps $D_0,D_1,D_2$ with $D_1D_0=D_2D_1=0$, and symmetric positive-definite mass maps $M_1,M_2$. Inputs are rational matrices $D_0,D_1,D_2,M_1,M_2$, rational initial coordinates $b_0\\in C^2$ and $d_{1/2}\\in(C^1)^*$, a positive rational step size $\\tau$, and an integer $N\\geq0$. The matrices and states use the fixed ordered bases and dual bases from that definition. All arithmetic is exact over $\\mathbb Q$; floating-point arithmetic is not part of this algorithm.\n\nFirst compute $R=M_1^{-1}$ once by fixed-order, no-row-swap Gauss–Jordan elimination of the augmented matrix $[M_1\\mid I_{n_1}]$. For each pivot column $j=1,\\ldots,n_1$, let $p$ be the current entry in position $(j,j)$. If $p=0$, return an inconsistency error. Otherwise divide row $j$ by $p$ and, for every $k\\neq j$, replace row $k$ by row $k$ minus its current column-$j$ entry times row $j$. After all columns are processed, let $R$ be the right block. For $n_1=0$, take the unique empty matrix for $R$ and perform no elimination.\n\nSet $(b,d):=(b_0,d_{1/2})$ and record this initial state. For each $n=0,\\ldots,N-1$, in the stated order compute\n\n$$b_{\\mathrm{new}}:=b-\\tau D_1Rd,\\qquad d_{\\mathrm{new}}:=d+\\tau D_1^{\\mathsf T}M_2b_{\\mathrm{new}}.$$\n\nAppend $(b_{\\mathrm{new}},d_{\\mathrm{new}})$ to the output list and then set $(b,d):=(b_{\\mathrm{new}},d_{\\mathrm{new}})$. After the loop, return $R$ together with the list of the $N+1$ states $(b_n,d_{n+1/2})$ for $0\\leq n\\leq N$. The error branch is unreachable on admissible inputs; the separate finite-step termination lemma proves this and the exact inverse output.",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
