            "alg-emg-fx-leapfrog-cochains": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-cm-positive-definite-matrix-square-roots": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-operator-norm-is-the-largest-singular-value": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "physical_scope": null,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Use finite-dimensional real cochain data as in the definition item and fix $\\tau>0$. For a state $(b,d)\\in\\mathcal X$, define the real one-step map $T_\\tau:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathcal X$ by\n\n$$b^+:=b-\\tau D_1M_1^{-1}d,\\qquad d^+:=d+\\tau D_1^{\\mathsf T}M_2b^+,\\qquad T_\\tau(b,d):=(b^+,d^+).$$\n\nOn rational admissible matrices, rational initial coordinates, and rational $\\tau>0$, this is the one-step update used by the exact rational algorithm; the algorithm's input domain is the rational one stated in its Definition. For every real input state, $D_2b^+=D_2b$ and $D_0^{\\mathsf T}d^+=D_0^{\\mathsf T}d$, so an input satisfying both zero constraints maps to a state satisfying both. Every continuously differentiable semidiscrete solution conserves $\\mathcal E(b,d)$. For any sequence $(b_n,d_{n+1/2})_{n\\geq0}$ with $(b_{n+1},d_{n+3/2})=T_\\tau(b_n,d_{n+1/2})$, define\n\n$$Q_\\tau(b,d):=b^{\\mathsf T}M_2b+d^{\\mathsf T}M_1^{-1}d-\\tau b^{\\mathsf T}M_2D_1M_1^{-1}d,\\qquad Q_n:=Q_\\tau(b_n,d_{n+1/2}).$$\n\nThen $Q_n$ is exactly invariant under $T_\\tau$. Equip coordinate and dual-coordinate columns with their standard Euclidean inner products, so $\\langle x,y\\rangle=x^{\\mathsf T}y$ and $\\lVert x\\rVert_2=(x^{\\mathsf T}x)^{1/2}$. Regard $S:=M_2^{1/2}D_1M_1^{-1/2}$ as a real linear map from the dual-coordinate columns of $(C^1)^*$ to the coordinate columns of $C^2$. Set $\\sigma:=0$ when its domain $(C^1)^*$ is zero; otherwise set $\\sigma:=\\max_{\\lVert y\\rVert_2=1}\\lVert Sy\\rVert_2$. For a nonzero domain this is the largest singular value of $S$. The quadratic form $Q_\\tau$ is positive definite on $\\mathcal X$ whenever $\\tau\\sigma<2$.",
