          "physical_scope": null,
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "In coordinates $(t,x,y,z)$ on Minkowski $\\mathbb R^4$ with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and orientation $dt\\wedge dx\\wedge dy\\wedge dz$, define $X_\\epsilon=-(F_\\epsilon)_{ab}(F_\\epsilon)^{ab}/4$ and $Y_\\epsilon=-(F_\\epsilon)_{ab}(\\star_\\eta F_\\epsilon)^{ab}/4$. The refuted assertion is: for every fixed finite $\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb R$ and every family $(F_\\epsilon)_{0<\\epsilon<1}$ of smooth exact real two-forms on $\\mathbb R^4$ (that is, $F_\\epsilon=dA_\\epsilon$ for a smooth real one-form $A_\\epsilon$), if $\\sup_{\\mathbb R^4}(|\\alpha X_\\epsilon|+|\\beta Y_\\epsilon|)\\to0$ as $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$, then there exist $C\\ge0$ and $\\epsilon_0\\in(0,1)$ such that $\\sup_{\\mathbb R^4}|\\partial_x(F_\\epsilon)_{tx}|\\le C$ for every $0<\\epsilon<\\epsilon_0$. The assertion says that small invariant corrections alone give a local uniform bound on this derivative scale.\n\n",
