                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1501.06570",
                "locator": "§4.4, equation (4.53), and §4.6.1, equations (4.54)–(4.56), classical charged-scalar mode flux context; the exact tensor normalization is stated locally."
              },
              {
                "title": "Visakan Balakumar, Aspects of quantum charged scalar field theory in curved spacetime: Black hole superradiance and Hadamard renormalisation, PhD thesis, complete text (2022)",
                "url": "https://inspirehep.net/files/c9d9d32ca091d580007a1fee1bb44dc0",
                "locator": "Chapter 3, §3.1 equations (3.1)–(3.8), charged scalar action, covariant derivative and Hilbert stress context; only the classical minimal-coupling tensor formula is used."
              }
            ]
          },
          "statement": "Let $(M,g,N,o)$ have the smooth, boundaryless, oriented and time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold structure, signature $(-,+,+,+)$, metric-selected connection $\\nabla^g$, and length-coordinate convention of [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]]. Let $A\\in\\Omega^1(M)$ be a supplied smooth real one-form, let $e\\in\\mathbb R$ be a fixed real coupling, and let $\\psi\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb C)$ be a smooth complex-valued function. In the length-coordinate convention, assign dimensionless components to $A_a$ and unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ to $e$, so $eA_a$ has derivative unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$; assign $\\psi$ an arbitrary fixed unit $[\\psi]$. Define the complex covector\n\n$$D_a\\psi:=\\nabla^g_a\\psi-ieA_a\\psi.$$\n\nSince $\\psi$ is a scalar, $\\nabla^g_a\\psi=\\partial_a\\psi$. Define the real symmetric covariant tensor\n\n$$\\mathsf T_{ab}[\\psi;g,A,e]:=\\overline{D_a\\psi}\\,D_b\\psi+\\overline{D_b\\psi}\\,D_a\\psi-g_{ab}g^{cd}\\overline{D_c\\psi}\\,D_d\\psi,$$\n\nwith indices raised using $g^{-1}$. Its covariant components have unit $[\\psi]^2\\mathrm m^{-2}$. This is a mathematical tensor assignment. Its interpretation as a stress-energy tensor, and any field equation or conservation law, require a separate physical model.\n\n"
        },
        {
