      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-gr-fx-adm-end",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "A specified asymptotically Euclidean ADM end",
          "reference": "items/def-gr-fx-adm-end.md",
          "design_state": "Step 1 authoring complete draft; readiness recorded separately.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "thm-gr-constraint-equations-in-normal-frame"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-gr-constraint-equations-in-normal-frame": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A fixed-chart geometric definition of an asymptotic energy limit; no Einstein constraint, positivity, coordinate-invariance, or empirical claim is included.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "For the geometric initial-data convention, let $(M,g)$ be a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold and let $\\iota:\\Sigma\\hookrightarrow M$ be a smooth embedded three-manifold whose induced metric $h:=\\iota^*g$ is positive definite. Its future unit normal $n$ satisfies $g(n,n)=-1$ and $g(n,X)=0$ for $X\\in T\\Sigma$. The second fundamental form is $K(X,Y):=g(\\nabla_Xn,Y)$, with $\\nabla$ the Levi-Civita connection of $g$ and the curvature sign convention stated in the Definition references section. The end data below are specified as a Riemannian metric and a symmetric tensor of these types; no embedding or Einstein constraint equation is required.\n\nLet $E=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:r>R_0\\}$, where $R_0>0$ is a length, $x=(x^1,x^2,x^3)$ are Cartesian coordinates measured in metres, and $r:=\\sqrt{(x^1)^2+(x^2)^2+(x^3)^2}$. Write $\\delta:=\\delta_{ij}\\,dx^i dx^j$ for the standard Euclidean metric, where $\\delta_{ij}=1$ if $i=j$ and $0$ otherwise; repeated spatial indices are summed over $1,2,3$. A **fixed-chart asymptotically Euclidean end** consists of this exterior domain, a $C^2$ symmetric positive-definite covariant $2$-tensor $h=h_{ij}\\,dx^i dx^j$, and a $C^1$ symmetric covariant $2$-tensor $K=K_{ij}\\,dx^i dx^j$ such that\n$$h_{ij}(x)=\\delta_{ij}+\\frac{a_{ij}}{r}+\\rho_{ij}(x),\\qquad K_{ij}(x)=O_1(r^{-2}),$$\nwhere $a_{ij}=a_{ji}$ is a constant real matrix, $\\epsilon>0$, and $\\rho=O_2(r^{-1-\\epsilon})$. For any tensor components $T_{ij}$, the notation $T=O_k(r^{-q})$ means that for every multi-index $\\alpha=(\\alpha_1,\\alpha_2,\\alpha_3)$ with $|\\alpha|:=\\alpha_1+\\alpha_2+\\alpha_3\\le k$ there are constants $C_\\alpha,R_\\alpha$ such that $|\\partial^\\alpha T_{ij}(x)|\\le C_\\alpha r^{-q-|\\alpha|}$ for $r\\ge R_\\alpha$, uniformly in angle. Thus the definition requires two Cartesian derivatives of the metric remainder and one of $K$. The coordinates and $r$ have units of metres; $\\delta_{ij}$ and $h_{ij}$ are dimensionless, $a_{ij}$ has units of metres, and $K$ has inverse-length units in a spatial orthonormal frame.\n\nFor $R>R_0$, the coordinate sphere $S_R:=\\{x:r=R\\}$ is a smooth embedded surface in $E$. Let $\\jmath_R:S_R\\hookrightarrow E$ be its inclusion and $\\gamma_R:=\\jmath_R^*\\delta$ its induced Riemannian metric. The Euclidean area measure $dS$ is the Riemannian area measure of $\\gamma_R$: for any smooth rank-two local parametrization $X:U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2\\to S_R$ with coordinates $(y^1,y^2)$, its coordinate density is\n$$dS=\\sqrt{\\det\\bigl[\\delta(\\partial_{y^a}X,\\partial_{y^b}X)\\bigr]_{a,b=1}^{2}}\\,dy^1dy^2.$$\n\nThe change-of-variables formula makes this density independent of the chosen surface coordinates; since $x$ is measured in metres, $dS$ has units of square metres. The sphere's outward Euclidean unit normal has components $n^i:=x^i/R$. If its limiting integral exists as $R\\to\\infty$—the spatial-infinity limit in this chosen chart—the **fixed-chart ADM energy** and its associated mass are\n$$E_{\\mathrm{ADM}}(h;x):=\\frac{c^4}{16\\pi G_N}\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_{S_R}\\bigl(\\partial_jh_{ij}-\\partial_ih_{jj}\\bigr)n^i\\,dS,\\qquad M_{\\mathrm{ADM}}:=\\frac{E_{\\mathrm{ADM}}}{c^2}.$$\nHere $c>0$ is the SI speed-of-light conversion constant with units metres per second, and $G_N>0$ is the Newtonian gravitational conversion constant with units metres cubed per kilogram per second squared. The limit has units of metres, $E_{\\mathrm{ADM}}$ has units of joules, and $M_{\\mathrm{ADM}}$ has units of kilograms. The prefactor fixes the asymptotic time-translation normalization; in SI coordinates use $x^0=ct$. No rescaling of this convention is part of the definition.\n",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Eric Gourgoulhon, 3+1 Formalism and Bases of Numerical Relativity, arXiv:gr-qc/0703035v1 (2007), §§7.2.1–7.2.2 and 7.3.1–7.3.2, PDF pp. 103–110; geometric units are used.",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/gr-qc/0703035"
              },
