          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-gr-constraint-equations-in-normal-frame": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "physical_scope": "A local differential-geometric identity for a Riemannian metric; it asserts no Einstein constraint or physical model.",
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $E\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be a nonempty open set with Cartesian coordinates $x^1,x^2,x^3$, standard Euclidean metric $\\delta=\\delta_{ij}\\,dx^i dx^j$ with $\\delta_{ij}=1$ for $i=j$ and $0$ otherwise, inverse components $\\delta^{ij}$, and Laplacian $\\Delta_\\delta=\\sum_{i=1}^3\\partial_i^2$. Let $u\\in C^\\infty(E,(0,\\infty))$ and set $h=u^4\\delta$. Form the product Lorentzian manifold $\\widehat M=\\mathbb R_\\tau\\times E$ with metric $g=-d\\tau^2+h$ and future orientation $\\partial_\\tau$; its slice $\\Sigma=\\{0\\}\\times E$ is a smooth spacelike hypersurface with induced metric $h$. Use the induced connection $D$ and curvature convention supplied for this slice by the earlier normal-decomposition theorem. In Cartesian coordinates define $R^\\ell{}_{bcd}$ by $R(\\partial_c,\\partial_d)\\partial_b=R^\\ell{}_{bcd}\\partial_\\ell$, $\\operatorname{Ric}^h_{ij}:=R^k{}_{ikj}$, and $S_h:=h^{ij}\\operatorname{Ric}^h_{ij}$. For $f=2\\log u$, write $|df|_\\delta^2:=\\delta^{ij}(\\partial_i f)(\\partial_j f)$. Then\n$$S_h=-8u^{-5}\\Delta_\\delta u.$$\n\nThis statement concerns a smooth positive conformal metric and its induced scalar curvature. The product construction only fixes the geometric convention; no Einstein equation, energy condition, or completeness hypothesis is imposed.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
