          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "statement": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice. Take the graph with three distinct vertices $u_1,u_2,v$, edges $a_1:u_1\\to v$, $a_2:u_2\\to v$ and their reverses $\\bar a_1,\\bar a_2$, and no other edges. Order the edges as $a_1<a_2<\\bar a_1<\\bar a_2$ and fix any vertex order. Assign the defining representation $\\rho(g)w:=gw$ on $V=\\mathbb C^2$, with its standard inner product, to $a_1,a_2$; assign the trivial representation on $\\mathbb C$ to each reverse edge. Thus $\\rho_{\\bar a_j}(g)z=z$ for every $g\\in SU(2)$ and $z\\in\\mathbb C$; the Haar projection's hypotheses impose no relation between representations assigned to reverse orientations.\n\nThe full vertex space is $W_v=V\\otimes V\\otimes\\mathbb C^*\\otimes\\mathbb C^*$. Write $\\mathbf 1^*(z)=z$ and identify $V\\otimes V$ with $W_v$ by the linear isomorphism $\\iota(\\xi)=\\xi\\otimes\\mathbf 1^*\\otimes\\mathbf 1^*$. For any basis $e_1,e_2$ of $V$, the nonzero tensor\n\n$$\\epsilon=e_1\\otimes e_2-e_2\\otimes e_1$$\n\nis invariant under $\\rho(g)\\otimes\\rho(g)$. Consequently the normalized Haar projection $P_v:W_v\\to W_v$ fixes $\\iota(\\epsilon)$; equivalently, $\\iota^{-1}P_v\\iota(\\epsilon)=\\epsilon$.\n\n",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
