          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $H$ be a finite-dimensional complex Hilbert space, let $I,J$ be finite nonempty sets, and let $(P_i)_{i\\in I}$ and $(Q_j)_{j\\in J}$ be projection-valued measures: $P_i=P_i^*=P_i^2$, $Q_j=Q_j^*=Q_j^2$, distinct projections in each family are orthogonal, and $\\sum_iP_i=\\sum_jQ_j=I_H$. A joint POVM is a family of effects $(G_{ij})_{(i,j)\\in I\\times J}$ with $\\sum_{i,j}G_{ij}=I_H$, $\\sum_jG_{ij}=P_i$, and $\\sum_iG_{ij}=Q_j$. Such a joint POVM exists exactly when $P_iQ_j=Q_jP_i$ for every $i,j$. When it exists it is unique and equals $G_{ij}=P_iQ_j$.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
