          "id": "def-qftx-rooted-forest-hopf",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Admissible-cut Hopf algebra for subdivergence forests",
          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal T$ be the set of isomorphism classes of finite nonempty rooted trees. A rooted tree is a finite connected acyclic graph with one distinguished vertex, the root; each edge is oriented from the vertex nearer the root (the parent) to the vertex farther from the root (the child). A forest is a finite multiset of rooted trees, including the empty forest $1$. Let $H=\\mathbb Q[\\mathcal T]$ be the commutative polynomial algebra generated by $\\mathcal T$; its monomial basis consists of forests, and the degree of a forest is its total number of vertices.\n\nFor a rooted tree $t$, an admissible cut is a subset $C$ of its edges meeting every root-to-leaf path in at most one edge. Removing the edges of $C$ leaves one component containing the root; denote it by $R_C(t)$. The components on the child side of the cut edges form a forest denoted by $P_C(t)$. For the empty edge cut, set $P_{\\varnothing}(t)=1$ and $R_{\\varnothing}(t)=t$. Define the coproduct on a tree generator by\n$$\\Delta(t)=t\\otimes1+\\sum_{C\\text{ admissible}}P_C(t)\\otimes R_C(t),$$\nwhere the sum includes the empty edge cut and retains multiplicities of distinct edge subsets. The term $t\\otimes1$ is the separate virtual full cut above the root. Extend $\\Delta$ multiplicatively to forests. Define $\\varepsilon(1)=1$ and $\\varepsilon(F)=0$ for every nonempty forest $F$. This specifies the rooted-tree subtraction model; it does not assert a canonical rooted-tree Hopf algebra or a graph-to-forest map for every Feynman graph.",
