          "title": "Laurent-series target and pole projection for subtraction",
          "statement": "Let $K$ be a commutative unital $\\mathbb Q$-algebra and let $\\epsilon$ be a formal indeterminate. The Laurent-series algebra $A=K((\\epsilon))$ consists of series $\\sum_{n\\ge N}a_n\\epsilon^n$ for some integer $N$, with coefficients in $K$. Put $A_-=\\epsilon^{-1}K[\\epsilon^{-1}]$ (finite sums of strictly negative powers) and $A_+=\\{\\sum_{n\\ge0}a_n\\epsilon^n:a_n\\in K\\}$ (formal series of nonnegative powers). Then $A=A_-\\oplus A_+$ as a $K$-module, and both summands are subalgebras, with $1\\in A_+$. Define $R:A\\to A_-$ by retaining exactly the strictly negative powers; $1-R$ is the projection onto $A_+$. The parameter $\\epsilon$ is formal and carries no physical or empirical interpretation in this definition.",
