          "title": "Admissible cuts give a connected graded Hopf algebra and recursive antipode",
          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal T$ be the isomorphism classes of finite nonempty rooted trees and $H=\\mathbb Q[\\mathcal T]$ their commutative polynomial algebra, graded by total vertices. An admissible cut is an edge subset meeting each root-to-leaf path at most once; $P_C(t)$ is its child-side forest and $R_C(t)$ its root-side tree. Use the coproduct $\\Delta(t)=t\\otimes1+\\sum_C P_C(t)\\otimes R_C(t)$, with the sum over admissible cuts including the empty cut, and counit $\\varepsilon(1)=1$, $\\varepsilon(F)=0$ for nonempty forests. The algebra $H$ is a connected graded bialgebra. Let $u$ be the algebra unit map and $m$ forest multiplication; use convolution $f*g=m(f\\otimes g)\\Delta$ on endomorphisms of $H$, with unit $u\\varepsilon$. It has a unique two-sided convolution inverse $S$ of its identity map. For every rooted tree $t$ of positive degree,\n$$S(1)=1,\\qquad S(t)=-t-\\sum_{\\substack{C\\ne\\varnothing\\\\ C\\text{ admissible}}}S(P_C(t))R_C(t).$$\nThe recursion is finite because each factor in every nonempty-cut term has fewer vertices than $t$. The antipode is multiplicative on forests. This is a theorem about the specified rooted-tree model and does not identify arbitrary Feynman graphs with rooted trees.",
