        {
          "id": "lem-qftx-brst-nilpotence-doublets",
          "kind": "lemma",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Nilpotence and contraction of nonminimal doublets",
          "statement": "The Abelian jet derivation $s$ defined in the local BRST algebra satisfies $s^2=0$. More generally, take any finite set of jet doublets $u^j,v^j$ with $|v^j|=|u^j|+1\\pmod 2$, $\\operatorname{gh}(v^j)=\\operatorname{gh}(u^j)+1$, $su^j=v^j$, and $sv^j=0$, and suppose the transformations of all other generators are independent of these doublets. Define the doublet-number operator\n$$N=\\sum_j\\left(u^j\\frac{\\partial_L}{\\partial u^j}+v^j\\frac{\\partial_L}{\\partial v^j}\\right)$$\nand the odd homotopy $h=\\sum_j u^j\\frac{\\partial_L}{\\partial v^j}$, using left graded derivatives. Then $[N,s]=0$ and $sh+hs=N$. Consequently the inclusion of the doublet-independent polynomial subalgebra induces an isomorphism on polynomial BRST cohomology. In particular, if $sa_k=0$ and $Na_k=ka_k$ for a positive integer $k$, then $a_k=s(ha_k/k)$.\n\nFor a ghost-number grading, write this cohomology as\n$$H^r(s,\\mathcal P)=\\ker(s:\\mathcal P^r\\to\\mathcal P^{r+1})/\\operatorname{im}(s:\\mathcal P^{r-1}\\to\\mathcal P^r).$$",
