          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "Vectorlike charges cancel cubic and linear sums",
          "statement": "Let $\\mathfrak g=\\mathbb R t$ be the one-dimensional Abelian real Lie algebra and take $V_+=V_-=\\mathbb C$. For $q\\in\\mathbb R$, define complex-linear representations by $\\rho_\\pm(zt)=\\pm qz\\,\\operatorname{id}_{\\mathbb C}$ for $z\\in\\mathbb C$, and attach the left-handed bookkeeping label to both. This opposite-charge pair is called vectorlike at the representation-data level here. It does not construct spinor fields or a charge-conjugation map; an interpretation as a field pair requires a separately specified field model and convention. The coefficient sums satisfy $q^3+(-q)^3=0$ and $q+(-q)=0$. With the no-half anticommutator convention $\\{A,B\\}=AB+BA$, the cubic coefficient is $d=2(q^3+(-q)^3)=0$, and the separate linear representation coefficient is $\\ell=q+(-q)=0$. This verifies cancellation of these two algebraic coefficients in that interface only; it does not identify them with a regulated anomaly, prove an all-orders theorem, or establish a nonperturbative gauge theory or cancellation of global anomalies.\n\n",
