          "id": "ex-qftx-dual-number-topological-model",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Nonsemisimple Frobenius sewing is also possible",
          "reference": "research/plan-quantum-field-theory-frontier-expansion-track.md#frobenius-sewing-and-exact-two-dimensional-topological-amplitudes",
          "design_state": "Assigned reserved design item; complete current statement and argument are recorded on the owning A/B page.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [
            "thm-closed-oriented-two-dimensional-tqfts-are-commutative-frobenius-algebras"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "thm-closed-oriented-two-dimensional-tqfts-are-commutative-frobenius-algebras": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $A=\\mathbb C[x]/(x^2)$ with basis $(1,x)$, unit $1$, multiplication inherited from the quotient, and Frobenius functional $\\epsilon(1)=0$, $\\epsilon(x)=1$. The bilinear pairing $b(a,b)=\\epsilon(ab)$ has matrix $\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$ in that basis, hence is nondegenerate. With the conjugate-linear involution fixing $x$, the form $h(a,b)=\\epsilon(a^*b)$ is Hermitian but indefinite. Here a finite-dimensional algebra is semisimple when its Jacobson radical (the intersection of its maximal left ideals) is zero; $A$ is not semisimple because $x$ is a nonzero nilpotent in that radical. The closed oriented classification therefore gives a closed two-dimensional TQFT from a nonsemisimple Frobenius algebra without asserting positive reflection form data.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-altered"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Joachim Kock, Frobenius algebras and 2D topological quantum field theories (short version)",
                "url": "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/Kock-FrobAlgTQFT-short.pdf",
                "locator": "§§2.2–2.4 and 3.1.12–3.1.13 (PDF pp. 22–30, 34–36), for Frobenius functionals, inverse-pairing comultiplication, and closed classification. The explicit dual-number matrix is checked locally."
              },
              {
                "title": "Joachim Kock, Frobenius Algebras and 2D Topological Quantum Field Theories (Cambridge book excerpt)",
                "url": "https://assets.cambridge.org/97805218/32670/excerpt/9780521832670_excerpt.pdf",
                "locator": "Excerpt pp. 1–7, for closed TQFTs from commutative Frobenius algebras; the dual-number calculation is supplied locally."
              },
              {
                "title": "Aaron D. Lauda and Hendryk Pfeiffer, Open-closed strings: Two-dimensional extended TQFTs and Frobenius algebras, arXiv:math/0510664v3",
                "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0510664v3",
                "locator": "§§3.3–3.7 and 4 (PDF pp. 18–42), for the closed-sector TQFT classification and its no-free-boundary convention."
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics",
          "strategy": "1.1 Every class in $A$ is uniquely $a+bx$ with $a,b\\in\\mathbb C$, and $(a+bx)(c+dx)=ac+(ad+bc)x$ because $x^2=0$. This is the quotient of the associative commutative algebra $\\mathbb C[x]$, so $A$ is a two-dimensional commutative unital associative algebra with nonzero nilpotent $x$. [given, algebra]\n\n1.2 The pairing values are $b(1,1)=0$, $b(1,x)=1$, $b(x,1)=1$, and $b(x,x)=0$, so its matrix is $\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$ and determinant $-1\\ne0$. Hence $b$ is nondegenerate. [given, algebra]\n\n2.1 Define $\\Delta(1)=1\\otimes x+x\\otimes1$ and $\\Delta(x)=x\\otimes x$. On both basis elements, $(\\epsilon\\otimes1)\\Delta=(1\\otimes\\epsilon)\\Delta=\\operatorname{id}_A$ as counit identities; the two coassociative composites on $1$ both equal $1\\otimes x\\otimes x+x\\otimes1\\otimes x+x\\otimes x\\otimes1$, and on $x$ both equal $x\\otimes x\\otimes x$. For the two Frobenius identities, bilinearity reduces to the four basis pairs $(1,1),(1,x),(x,1),(x,x)$; on these pairs all three composites $\\Delta\\mu$, $(1\\otimes\\mu)(\\Delta\\otimes1)$, and $(\\mu\\otimes1)(1\\otimes\\Delta)$ respectively equal $\\Delta(1),\\Delta(x),\\Delta(x),0$. Thus the specified maps make $A$ a commutative Frobenius algebra. [given, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n2.2 The ideal $(x)$ is maximal because $A/(x)\\cong\\mathbb C$. For every maximal ideal $M$, the quotient $A/M$ is a field and the image of $x$ there is nilpotent because $x^2=0$; a field has no nonzero nilpotents, so $x\\in M$. Hence $0\\ne x\\in J(A)$, the intersection of all maximal left ideals, and $A$ is not semisimple under the stated definition. [given, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.3 For $a=a_0+a_1x$ and $b=b_0+b_1x$, $h(a,b)=\\overline{a_0}b_1+\\overline{a_1}b_0$, so $h(a,b)=\\overline{h(b,a)}$ and the form is Hermitian. Also $h(1+x,1+x)=\\epsilon((1+x)^2)=2$ while $h(1-x,1-x)=\\epsilon((1-x)^2)=-2$, so it takes both signs and is not positive definite. [given, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n3.1 By step 2.1 the algebra is finite-dimensional, commutative, and Frobenius, so [F1] applies over $k=\\mathbb C$ and gives the closed oriented two-dimensional TQFT. This conclusion uses the closed category only and adds no open/closed or positivity claim. [given, F1, step 2.1] ∎",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "verification": {}
        }
      ],
