a_0$, and then the identity on the common underlying cell data $\\mathcal C_G(-\\Sigma_0)=\\mathcal C_G(\\Sigma_0)$. The outgoing functor $r_1$ is defined by restriction and its specified identification with $\\Sigma_1$. In either transport, a chosen edge mapped to the opposite chosen direction uses the inverse-edge rule from the flat-connection Definition. These functors give a span of finite groupoids\n\n$$\\mathcal C_G(\\Sigma_0)\\xleftarrow{r_0}\\mathcal C_G(M)\\xrightarrow{r_1}\\mathcal C_G(\\Sigma_1).$$\n\nThe associated linear map is\n\n$$Z_M:=(r_1)_!r_0^*:V_G(\\Sigma_0)\\to V_G(\\Sigma_1),$$\n\nwhere pullback and pushforward are the finite homotopy-fiber operations just defined. For empty boundary, the amplitude is $Z_G(M)=|\\mathcal C_G(M)|$.\n\n",
