          "title": "Finite-spin product and local topologies",
          "statement": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$, a finite nonempty alphabet $S$ with the discrete topology, and a bijective enumeration $x_1,x_2,\\ldots$ of $\\mathbb Z^d$. Let $\\Omega=S^{\\mathbb Z^d}$ be the set of all configurations $\\sigma:\\mathbb Z^d\\to S$ with the product topology, the smallest topology that makes each coordinate map $\\sigma\\mapsto\\sigma_x$ continuous. For a finite set $F\\subseteq\\mathbb Z^d$ and assignment $a\\in S^F$, the cylinder set determined by $(F,a)$ is $[F,a]=\\{\\sigma\\in\\Omega:\\sigma|_F=a\\}$. The cylinder sigma-algebra $\\mathcal C$ is generated by these sets.\n\nFor each finite $F$, let $\\pi_F:\\Omega\\to S^F$ be restriction to $F$. A probability measure $\\rho$ on $(\\Omega,\\mathcal C)$ is a countably additive map $\\mathcal C\\to[0,1]$ with $\\rho(\\varnothing)=0$ and $\\rho(\\Omega)=1$. Its finite-coordinate pushforward is the measure on all subsets of $S^F$ defined by $(\\pi_F)_*\\rho(B):=\\rho(\\pi_F^{-1}(B))$; the preimage is a finite union of cylinders and belongs to $\\mathcal C$. For probability measures $\\mu_n,\\mu$ on this space, local weak convergence $\\mu_n\\to_{\\mathrm{loc}}\\mu$ means that $(\\pi_F)_*\\mu_n(B)\\to(\\pi_F)_*\\mu(B)$ for every finite $F\\subseteq\\mathbb Z^d$ and every $B\\subseteq S^F$. Equivalently, all finite-coordinate cylinder probabilities converge. The cylinder sigma-algebra is shown below in a local result to equal the Borel sigma-algebra, with local weak convergence equal to ordinary weak convergence on this space.",
