          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Locally finite counting measures and vague topology",
          "statement": "Fix $d\\ge1$. Let $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^d)$ be the $\\sigma$-algebra generated by the Euclidean open sets, and understand all measures below as defined on $(\\mathbb R^d,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^d))$. A measure here is a countably additive map from $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^d)$ to $[0,\\infty]$ that assigns $0$ to the empty set. A locally finite counting measure on $\\mathbb R^d$ is a measure of the form $\\xi=\\sum_{r\\in I}\\delta_{x_r}$, where $I$ is finite or countable, repetitions among the points $x_r\\in\\mathbb R^d$ are allowed, and every compact set contains only finitely many listed points, counting repetitions. The empty sum is allowed and is the zero measure. For a Borel set $B$, $\\xi(B)$ is the number of listed points in $B$, with multiplicity. Write $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{lf}}(\\mathbb R^d)$ for the set of these measures. For $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$, the point mass $\\delta_x$ assigns mass $1$ to a Borel set containing $x$ and mass $0$ otherwise.\n\nFor any continuous compactly supported $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$ and $\\xi=\\sum_{r\\in I}\\delta_{x_r}$, define $\\int f\\,d\\xi:=\\sum_{r:x_r\\in\\operatorname{supp}f}f(x_r)$, where $\\operatorname{supp}f$ is the closure of $\\{x:f(x)\\ne0\\}$. This sum is finite by local finiteness, counts multiplicities, and is independent of the listing because $\\xi$ fixes the multiplicity at every point. For the zero measure it is $0$.\n\nThe vague topology on $\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{lf}}(\\mathbb R^d)$ is the coarsest topology for which $\\xi\\mapsto\\int f\\,d\\xi$ is continuous for every nonnegative continuous compactly supported function $f:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R$. Thus $\\xi_n\\to_{\\mathrm v}\\xi$ exactly when $\\int f\\,d\\xi_n\\to\\int f\\,d\\xi$ for every such $f$.\n\nGiven a probability space $(\\Theta,\\mathcal A,\\mathbb P)$, meaning that $\\mathcal A$ is a $\\sigma$-algebra on $\\Theta$ and $\\mathbb P$ is a countably additive probability measure on $\\mathcal A$ with $\\mathbb P(\\Theta)=1$, a random locally finite counting measure is an $(\\mathcal A,\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm v})$-measurable map $\\Xi:\\Theta\\to\\Gamma_{\\mathrm{lf}}(\\mathbb R^d)$, where $\\mathcal B_{\\mathrm v}:=\\sigma\\{U:U\\text{ is open in the vague topology just defined}\\}$. We require that, for every bounded Borel set $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$, the evaluation $\\Xi(B):\\Theta\\to[0,\\infty)$ given by $\\Xi(B)(\\theta)=\\Xi(\\theta)(B)$ is $\\mathcal A$-measurable; it is finite by local finiteness. Write $\\mathbb E_{\\mathbb P}$ for integration with respect to $\\mathbb P$. A sequence $(\\Xi_n)$ of such measures on a common probability space uses this same domain and target for every $n$. If $K$ is compact, its local counts are uniformly integrable on $K$ when\n\n$$\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\sup_n\\mathbb E_{\\mathbb P}\\!\\left[\\Xi_n(K)\\mathbf1_{\\{\\Xi_n(K)>R\\}}\\right]=0.$$\n\nThis is the count-tail condition used when an argument needs control of unbounded observables supported in $K$. The definition asserts no convergence of expectations without the exact test-function, support, and tail hypotheses of the result being applied.",
