          "id": "ex-csm-frontier-singular-local-limit",
          "kind": "example",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "A fixed label vanishes locally while a spatial mark stays random",
          "statement": "For each integer $n\\ge1$, put $S_n=\\{-n,-n+1,\\ldots,n-1\\}\\subset\\mathbb Z$ and $L_n=\\{1,2,\\ldots,2n\\}$. Let $\\Omega_n$ be the finite set of bijections $\\pi:L_n\\to S_n$, equipped with its power-set sigma-algebra and probability $\\mathbb P_n$ defined by $\\mathbb P_n(\\{\\pi\\})=1/(2n)!$ for every $\\pi\\in\\Omega_n$ (and by summing these atom masses on every subset); let $\\pi_n$ be the identity random bijection on this probability space. The position of fixed label $1$ is $U_n=\\pi_n(1)$, and its point measure is $\\Xi_n=\\delta_{U_n}$. Give labels $1,\\ldots,n$ mark $0$ and labels $n+1,\\ldots,2n$ mark $1$. Let $V_n$ be the mark of the particle occupying spatial site $0$. For every nonnegative continuous compactly supported $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ and every $\\varepsilon>0$,\n$$\\mathbb P_n\\!\\left(\\left|\\int f\\,d\\Xi_n\\right|>\\varepsilon\\right)\\longrightarrow0,$$\nwhile $\\mathbb P_n(V_n=0)=\\mathbb P_n(V_n=1)=1/2$ for every $n$. The testwise convergence assertion uses the varying-space convention displayed here and requires no coupling between different $n$.",
