          "id": "def-csm-frontier-infinite-volume-criticality",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Infinite-volume nonanalytic-pressure criticality",
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open interval of values of a scalar control parameter $\\theta$, expressed in one fixed unit if it is dimensional. A function $g:I\\to\\mathbb R$ is **real analytic** if for every $\\theta_0\\in I$ there are $\\epsilon>0$ and real coefficients $(a_k)_{k\\ge0}$ such that $(\\theta_0-\\epsilon,\\theta_0+\\epsilon)\\subseteq I$, the series $\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}a_k(\\theta-\\theta_0)^k$ converges there, and its sum equals $g(\\theta)$ throughout that interval. Let $(N_n)_{n\\ge1}$ be positive integers with $N_n\\to\\infty$, interpreted as finite system sizes such as site counts. A finite-volume partition-function sequence on $I$ is a sequence of real-analytic functions $Z_n:I\\to(0,\\infty)$. For each $n$, define its dimensionless log partition density per site by $p_n(\\theta):=N_n^{-1}\\log Z_n(\\theta)$, where $\\log$ is the natural logarithm. If the pointwise limit $p(\\theta):=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}p_n(\\theta)$ exists as a finite real number for every $\\theta\\in I$, call $p$ the infinite-volume pressure associated with this sequence. A point $\\theta_c\\in I$ is critical under the nonanalytic-pressure criterion exactly when there do not exist $\\epsilon>0$ with $(\\theta_c-\\epsilon,\\theta_c+\\epsilon)\\subseteq I$ and real coefficients $(a_k)_{k\\ge0}$ such that $\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}a_k(\\theta-\\theta_c)^k$ converges and equals $p(\\theta)$ throughout that interval.\n\nThis definition specifies only the nonanalytic-pressure criterion and makes no claim that a particular model satisfies it.\n\n",
