          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Correctness of finite Gaussian block elimination",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ and the hypotheses of [[alg-csm-frontier-gaussian-block-rg]] with input $K_0$ and retained sets $R_0,\\ldots,R_q$. Let $K_j,C_j,\\ell_j$ be the algorithm outputs. At each stage write $E_j=R_{j-1}\\setminus R_j$, and use the empty-set conventions of the algorithm. Then the algorithm terminates after $q$ stages, every nonempty retained precision $K_j$ is positive definite, and for every fixed retained vector $x\\in\\mathbb R^{R_q}$,\n\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^{\\bigcup_{j=1}^q E_j}}\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac12 \\widetilde z(x,y)^{\\mathsf T}K_0\\widetilde z(x,y)\\right)\\,d\\lambda(y) =\\exp\\!\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^q\\ell_j\\right)\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac12x^{\\mathsf T}K_qx\\right),$$\n\nwhere $y$ lists the eliminated coordinates in increasing order and $\\widetilde z(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^{R_0}$ is the unique full coordinate vector agreeing with $x$ on $R_q$ and with $y$ on $R_0\\setminus R_q$. The measure $\\lambda$ is Euclidean Lebesgue measure when the eliminated-coordinate space has positive dimension; if $\\bigcup_jE_j=\\varnothing$, identify $\\mathbb R^{\\bigcup_jE_j}$ with the singleton $\\mathbb R^0=\\{()\\}$ and let $\\lambda$ assign mass one to it. If $R_q\\ne\\varnothing$, the normalized marginal law of the retained coordinates under $\\gamma_{K_0}$ is exactly $\\gamma_{K_q}$; if $R_q=\\varnothing$, the final retained space is the singleton $\\mathbb R^0$ and the identity gives $Z_{K_0}=\\exp(\\sum_j\\ell_j)$. In all cases,\n\n$$\\ln Z_{K_0}=\\sum_{j=1}^q\\ell_j+\\ln Z_{K_q},$$\n\nwith $Z_{K_q}=1$ under the empty-space convention. These are exact finite-dimensional Gaussian identities and make no approximation or empirical claim.\n\n",
