          "title": "Finite Potts and Fortuin–Kasteleyn weights",
          "statement": "Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph, so $V$ is finite and $E$ is a set of two-element subsets of $V$. Fix an integer $Q\\geq2$, write $[Q]=\\{1,\\ldots,Q\\}$, and assign to each edge $e\\in E$ a finite activity $v_e\\in[0,\\infty)$. A spin configuration is a function $\\sigma:V\\to[Q]$. Its unnormalized Potts weight and partition function are\n$$w_P(\\sigma):=\\prod_{e=\\{u_e,w_e\\}\\in E}\\bigl(1+v_e\\mathbf1_{\\{\\sigma(u_e)=\\sigma(w_e)\\}}\\bigr),\\qquad Z_P(Q,\\mathbf v):=\\sum_{\\sigma:V\\to[Q]}w_P(\\sigma).$$\nFor $A\\subseteq E$, let $G_A=(V,A)$, and let $k_G(A)$ be its number of connected components, counting isolated vertices; equivalently, these are the equivalence classes generated by the endpoint pairs of edges in $A$. Put $v_A:=\\prod_{e\\in A}v_e$, with empty product $1$. The unnormalized Fortuin–Kasteleyn (FK) weight and partition function are\n$$w_{FK}(A):=Q^{k_G(A)}v_A,\\qquad Z_{FK}(Q,\\mathbf v):=\\sum_{A\\subseteq E}w_{FK}(A).$$\nBoth normalizers are finite and positive: the spin sum is finite with every term at least $1$, while the empty-edge FK term is $Q^{|V|}>0$. These are finite mathematical weights; no physical units or empirical status are assigned.",
