          "title": "Normalized SSEP macroscopic fluctuation action",
          "statement": "Fix an integer $d\\ge1$ and let $\\mathbb T^d=(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^d$ carry normalized product Haar measure $dx$, with $dx(\\mathbb T^d)=1$. Coordinates and time are dimensionless in the diffusive normalization $D=1$. For $r\\in(0,1)$ set the mobility function $\\chi(r):=r(1-r)$. For any finite interval $[a,b]$ with $a<b$, let $\\rho:[a,b]\\times\\mathbb T^d\\to\\mathbb R$ be a scalar density and let $j:[a,b]\\times\\mathbb T^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a vector current. Require both fields to be smooth up to the time endpoints and periodic in space, and require that for some $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1/2)$, $\\varepsilon\\le\\rho(t,x)\\le1-\\varepsilon$ for every $(t,x)$. Call $(\\rho,j)$ admissible when it also satisfies the continuity equation $\\partial_t\\rho+\\nabla\\cdot j=0$. Define its dimensionless action by\n\n$$\\mathcal I_{[a,b]}(\\rho,j):=\\frac14\\int_a^b\\int_{\\mathbb T^d}\\frac{|j(t,x)+\\nabla\\rho(t,x)|^2}{\\rho(t,x)(1-\\rho(t,x))}\\,dx\\,dt.$$\n\nThis is the macroscopic fluctuation functional in the stated normalized SSEP coefficient convention. It is a definition of a functional and does not assert a microscopic path-space large-deviation principle or a probability estimate.",
