          "title": "Periodic hard-sphere Boltzmann–Grad scaling convention",
          "statement": "Fix an integer dimension $d\\ge2$, a period length $L>0$ metres, and an equal particle mass $m>0$ kilograms. The position space is the flat torus $\\mathbb T_L^d:=(\\mathbb R/L\\mathbb Z)^d$. For lifts $x,y\\in\\mathbb R^d$ of points $[x],[y]\\in\\mathbb T_L^d$, define\n\n$$\\operatorname{dist}_{\\mathbb T_L^d}([x],[y]):=\\min_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d}|x-y+Lk|,$$\n\nwhere $|z|=(\\sum_{\\ell=1}^d z_\\ell^2)^{1/2}$ is the Euclidean norm. For $q_i=[x]$ and $q_j=[y]$, the vectors $x-y+Lk$, $k\\in\\mathbb Z^d$, are the displacement representatives from $q_j$ to $q_i$. The minimum is attained because $|x-y+Lk|\\to\\infty$ as $|k|\\to\\infty$, and its value is independent of the chosen lifts because changing lifts reindexes $k$. For each $N\\ge2$ under consideration, let $\\delta_N>0$ be the physical sphere diameter in metres and put $a_N:=\\delta_N/L$. An admissible configuration $(q_1,\\ldots,q_N)$ has pairwise distances $\\operatorname{dist}_{\\mathbb T_L^d}(q_i,q_j)>\\delta_N$. For a hard-sphere trajectory below, require $\\delta_N<L/2$; this restriction holds eventually in the scaling prescription. If two quotient displacement representatives of length $\\delta_N$ existed, their difference would be a nonzero vector in $L\\mathbb Z^d$, of length at least $L$, but the triangle inequality bounds its length by $2\\delta_N<L$; thus the contact normal is unique.\n\nA hard-sphere trajectory, when defined, is specified on one interval $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ with nonempty interior, with time $t\\in I$ measured in seconds. For each $i=1,\\ldots,N$ its maps are\n$$q_i:I\\longrightarrow\\mathbb T_L^d,\\qquad v_i:I\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^d,$$\nwhere $v_i$ is measured in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. Require $\\operatorname{dist}_{\\mathbb T_L^d}(q_i(t),q_j(t))\\ge\\delta_N$ for every $t\\in I$ and $i\\ne j$. The interval may be bounded or unbounded and may include either finite endpoint. Positions are continuous on $I$. Define the interior contact-time set\n\n$$C:=\\{t\\in I^\\circ:\\operatorname{dist}_{\\mathbb T_L^d}(q_i(t),q_j(t))=\\delta_N\\text{ for some }i<j\\}.$$\n\nRequire $C$ to be locally finite in $I^\\circ$: every compact interval $[a,b]\\subset I^\\circ$ contains only finitely many elements of $C$. Each $t_c\\in C$ must be a binary contact as defined below. In particular, there is an $\\eta>0$ with $(t_c-\\eta,t_c+\\eta)\\subset I^\\circ$ and $C\\cap(t_c-\\eta,t_c+\\eta)=\\{t_c\\}$; this is the meaning of an isolated contact. All pairwise distances are strictly greater than $\\delta_N$ at interior times outside $C$. A free-flight interval is a maximal open subinterval of $I^\\circ$ containing no point of $C$. On each such interval require every $q_i$ and $v_i$ to be $C^1$ and to satisfy $\\dot q_i=v_i$ and $\\dot v_i=0$. Here $\\dot q_i=v_i$ means that any local Euclidean lift of $q_i$ has derivative $v_i$; changing that lift by a constant lattice vector leaves the derivative unchanged.\n\nAt an interior contact time use both one-sided velocity limits and set $v_i(t_c)=v_i^+$, the outgoing value. A finite endpoint is included only when the corresponding limits from within $I$ exist, and the position and velocity maps take those values there. No collision rule involving a missing side of an endpoint is imposed; a two-sided update there would require an extension of the trajectory through that time. Contact times may accumulate only at endpoints of $I$ or at infinity; included finite endpoints still require the stated one-sided limits. These conventions specify a partial trajectory only and assert no continuation through a collision accumulation. A binary contact of particles $i,j$ at an interior time $t_c\\in I^\\circ$ means $\\operatorname{dist}_{\\mathbb T_L^d}(q_i(t_c),q_j(t_c))=\\delta_N$, while no other pair is in contact at $t_c$. Write $v_i^-$ and $v_i^+$ for the one-sided velocity limits at the contact. Let $n$ be the unique unit vector from the centre of $j$ to the centre of $i$ along the shortest quotient displacement. All particles other than $i,j$ retain their velocities at this contact. The equal-mass elastic reflection rule is\n\n$$v_i^+=v_i^- -\\bigl((v_i^--v_j^-)\\cdot n\\bigr)n,\\qquad v_j^+=v_j^- +\\bigl((v_i^--v_j^-)\\cdot n\\bigr)n.$$\n\nThe Boltzmann–Grad scaling prescription is a sequence, indexed by all sufficiently large integers $N$, with $N\\to\\infty$, $a_N\\to0$, and\n\n$$N a_N^{d-1}=\\kappa$$\n\nfor a fixed dimensionless $\\kappa\\in(0,\\infty)$. Under this exact relation, $a_N=(\\kappa/N)^{1/(d-1)}\\to0$, so the trajectory restriction $\\delta_N<L/2$ holds for every sufficiently large $N$. The factor $a_N$ is the diameter measured relative to the fixed box length, so the displayed parameter is dimensionless. This is a definition of a finite hard-sphere model and a scaling prescription only; it asserts neither global existence of the hard-sphere flow nor convergence to a Boltzmann equation.",
