          "statement": "Let $n\\ge2$ be an integer and let $C\\subseteq[0,1]^n$ be coordinatewise increasing and a finite union of axis-parallel boxes, with specified open or closed endpoint conventions. Degenerate boxes are allowed. Let $v_r$ denote elementary $r$-dimensional box volume: a box has the product of its side lengths as volume, and finite unions are evaluated by a common coordinate-endpoint subdivision, with coordinate faces assigned volume zero. Put $f=\\mathbf1_C$, $a=v_n(C)$, and\n\n$$F_i=v_{n-1}\\{x_{-i}\\in[0,1]^{n-1}:f(x_{-i},1)-f(x_{-i},0)=1\\},\\qquad M=\\max_{1\\le i\\le n}F_i.$$\n\nIf $M=0$, then $a(1-a)=0$. If $M>0$, then\n\n$$a(1-a)\\le\\frac{2\\sum_{i=1}^nF_i}{1+\\log(1/M)}.$$\n\nIn every case,\n\n$$\\max_{1\\le i\\le n}F_i\\ge\\frac14a(1-a)\\frac{\\log n}{n}.$$\n\nLogarithms are natural. Endpoint influence uses the actual values on the faces. The assertion and proof use only finite box volumes, finite probability spaces and elementary real calculus; they require no countable product measure or choice principle.",
