          "dependency_level": 2,
          "library": "physics",
          "statement": "For an integer $m\\geq1$, let $T_m$ be the labelled square cellulation obtained by quotienting the unit square cellulation of $\\mathbb R^2$ by $m\\mathbb Z^2$. Its vertices are $V=(\\mathbb Z/m\\mathbb Z)^2$. For every $(i,j)\\in V$, retain a horizontal edge label $h(i,j)$ from $(i,j)$ to $(i+1,j)$, a vertical edge label $v(i,j)$ from $(i,j)$ to $(i,j+1)$, and a square face label $f(i,j)$. All indices are modulo $m$. Distinct labels remain distinct even when their endpoints agree: for $m=2$ this retains parallel edges, and for $m=1$ it retains two loops. In a face boundary retain its four edge occurrences before reducing coefficients modulo two. Thus $|V|=|F|=m^2$ and $|E|=2m^2$. The dual has vertices $f(i,j)$ and a separate label $e^*$ crossing each primal label $e$; specifically $h(i,j)^*$ joins $f(i,j-1)$ to $f(i,j)$ and $v(i,j)^*$ joins $f(i-1,j)$ to $f(i,j)$. Its faces correspond to primal vertices and use the dual square cellulation.\n\nLet $\\mathbb F_2=\\{0,1\\}$ be the field with addition and multiplication modulo two. Let $C_0=\\mathbb F_2^V$, $C_1=\\mathbb F_2^E$, and $C_2=\\mathbb F_2^F$ have the indicated labelled bases. Define $\\partial_1:C_1\\to C_0$ by summing the two endpoint occurrences of each edge, so a loop has zero boundary, and define $\\partial_2:C_2\\to C_1$ by\n\n$$\\partial_2 f(i,j)=h(i,j)+v(i+1,j)+h(i,j+1)+v(i,j).$$\n\nThen $\\partial_1\\partial_2=0$ and $H:=\\ker\\partial_1/\\operatorname{im}\\partial_2$ has dimension two. For $A\\subseteq E$, let $C_1(A)$ be the coordinate subspace generated by its labels, put $Z(A)=C_1(A)\\cap\\ker\\partial_1$, and define\n\n$$r(A):=\\dim_{\\mathbb F_2}\\operatorname{im}\\bigl(Z(A)\\longrightarrow H\\bigr).$$\n\nLet $k(A)$ count all components of the spanning graph $(V,A)$, including isolated vertices. Let $A^*=\\{e^*:e\\in E\\setminus A\\}$, and let $k^*(A^*)$ count all components of this dual spanning graph. Define $r^*(A^*)$ by the same matrix construction on the dual cellu