          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Use the standard inner product on $\\mathbb C^2$ and its ordered basis $|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle$. For $i,j\\in\\{0,1\\}$, let $E_{ij}\\in M_2(\\mathbb C)$ have entry $1$ in row $i$, column $j$, and entry $0$ elsewhere, with rows and columns ordered $0,1$. For each integer $N\\ge2$, index the factors cyclically modulo $N$ and let $A^0=|0\\rangle\\langle0|=E_{00}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$ and $A^1=|1\\rangle\\langle1|=E_{11}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$. The periodic trace contraction has amplitude $\\operatorname{tr}(A^{i_1}\\cdots A^{i_N})$ on basis vector $|i_1\\cdots i_N\\rangle$ and equals $|0^N\\rangle+|1^N\\rangle$; after normalization it is the GHZ vector $(|0^N\\rangle+|1^N\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$. The transfer map fixes $E_{00}$ and $E_{11}$ and annihilates $E_{01}$ and $E_{10}$. With $\\rho=I_2/2$ and $\\ell=I_2$, the associated infinite transfer state has $\\omega(Z_1)=0$ and $\\omega(Z_1I^{\\otimes g}Z_{g+2})=1$ for every integer gap $g\\ge0$, where $Z=|0\\rangle\\langle0|-|1\\rangle\\langle1|$ acts on one site. Thus its connected $Z$ correlation is exactly $1$ at every gap and does not decay.",
