          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K$ be a finite cellulation of a closed, connected, orientable surface of genus $g\\ge0$, with the incidence and Hamiltonian conventions of the toric-code definition. For positive couplings $J_v,K_f$, the Hamiltonian is positive and frustration free, meaning its positive penalty terms have a common nonzero vector in their kernels. Its ground-space dimension is $2^{2g}$. Define its spectral gap as the least positive eigenvalue of $H_K$, or $+\\infty$ if there is no positive eigenvalue. This gap is at least $2m$, where $m=\\min(\\{J_v:v\\in V\\}\\cup\\{K_f:f\\in F\\})>0$, and in particular is at least $m$. On a uniform $L\\times L$ square torus with $L\\ge3$ and couplings $J_v=J>0$, $K_f=K>0$, the exact gap is $2\\min(J,K)$.",
