          "physical_scope": "Pure finite-dimensional representation theory of the explicit algebra $D(G)$; no modular-functor, braided-category, or physical-realization claim.",
          "statement": "Let $G$ be a finite group and let $D(G)$ be the finite-dimensional complex algebra with basis $d_{h,g}=\\delta_h\\otimes g$ and multiplication $d_{h,g}d_{k,l}=\\mathbf 1_{h=gkg^{-1}}d_{h,gl}$. A finite-dimensional representation of $D(G)$ means a nonzero finite-dimensional complex vector space $V$ with a unital algebra homomorphism $D(G)\\to\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$; it is irreducible when it has no nonzero proper invariant subspace. Two such representations are equivalent if an invertible complex-linear map intertwines their $D(G)$-actions. For $c\\in G$, define its conjugacy class $C_c=\\{gcg^{-1}:g\\in G\\}$ and centralizer $Z_G(c)=\\{z\\in G:zc=cz\\}$. The equivalence classes of irreducible finite-dimensional representations of $D(G)$ are in bijection with pairs $(C,[\\pi])$, where $C$ is a conjugacy class, $c\\in C$ is any chosen representative, and $[\\pi]$ is an equivalence class of irreducible finite-dimensional complex representations of $Z_G(c)$. In this statement, each such equivalence class is a **sector**. The representation indexed by $(C,[\\pi])$ has dimension $|C|\\dim\\pi$, independently of the chosen representative up to the corresponding conjugation of the centralizer representation. The sum of the squared dimensions of all these irreducible representations is $|G|^2=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}D(G)$.",
