          "statement": "Let $d\\geq1$ and let $(T_t)_{t\\geq0}$ be complex-linear maps on $M_d(\\mathbb C)$ that are completely positive (each finite ancilla amplification preserves positive matrices) and trace-preserving ($\\operatorname{Tr}(T_t(X))=\\operatorname{Tr}X$ for every $X$), with $T_0=\\operatorname{id}$ and $T_{t+s}=T_t\\circ T_s$ for all $s,t\\geq0$. A density matrix $\\rho$ means $\\rho=\\rho^*$, $v^*\\rho v\\geq0$ for every $v\\in\\mathbb C^d$, and $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$; it is faithful when $v^*\\rho v>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb C^d$. For a Hermitian matrix $H\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ and $\\beta\\in\\mathbb R$, define $Z_\\beta:=\\operatorname{Tr}(e^{-\\beta H})>0$ and the Gibbs density $\\sigma_\\beta:=e^{-\\beta H}/Z_\\beta$. A density matrix $\\sigma$ is stationary for $(T_t)$ when $T_t(\\sigma)=\\sigma$ for every $t\\geq0$. For a density matrix $\\rho$ and faithful density $\\sigma$, let $D(\\rho\\|\\sigma):=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho\\log\\rho)-\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho\\log\\sigma)$, using the natural logarithm, the finite spectral logarithm on the support of $\\rho$, and $0\\log0:=0$. The claim refuted here is that nonincrease of relative entropy to a faithful stationary Gibbs density is sufficient to guarantee that the Gibbs density is the unique stationary density and that every initial density converges to it.\n\n",
