          "statement": "Take the finite-chain data of the current definition on $\\mathcal H_L$ and identify $\\mathcal H:=\\mathcal H_L$. Let $H=H^*$ have energy unit joules, let $\\hbar>0$ have unit joule-seconds, and let $\\rho$ be a positive-definite trace-one matrix satisfying $[\\rho,H]=0$. Define $\\omega(A):=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho A)$ and $\\alpha_t(A):=e^{iHt/\\hbar}Ae^{-iHt/\\hbar}$. Let $J_L$ be the total lattice current of the finite-volume current definition and center it with respect to this same $\\rho$, setting $J:=J_L-\\omega(J_L)I$. With no identification of $H$ and $H_L$, the estimate below is an abstract bound for this observable under $\\alpha_t$. Its lattice-transport specialization further takes $H=H_L$; then the Gibbs density $\\rho_{\\beta,L}$ in that definition is stationary and is an admissible choice of $\\rho$. More generally, $\\rho_{\\beta,L}$ can be used with another $H$ exactly when it also satisfies $[\\rho_{\\beta,L},H]=0$. Then $J=J^*$, as established by the local continuity result. For $m\\ge1$, let $Q_1,\\ldots,Q_m$ be linearly independent conserved matrices, $[H,Q_a]=0$. Put\n$$G_{ab}:=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho Q_a^*Q_b),\\qquad v_a:=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho Q_a^*J).$$\nThen $G$ is positive definite, the finite Cesàro limit exists, and\n$$\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac1T\\int_0^T\\operatorname{Re}\\,\\omega\\!\\left(\\alpha_t(J)J\\right)\\,dt\\;\\ge\\;v^*G^{-1}v\\;\\ge0.$$\nThis is a finite-dimensional persistent-correlation bound. It does not identify a thermodynamic Drude weight or assert that either volume/time order of limits exists.\n\n",
