          "physical_scope": null,
          "statement": "Fix a spatial normalization and current convention as in the finite-volume lattice-current definition. Let $C:[0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ be a real, even, connected autocorrelation for that specified normalized current. In the classical alternative, let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space and let $t\\mapsto J(t)$ be a norm-continuous map into the real Hilbert space $L^2(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$. Assume it is centered and second-order stationary, meaning $\\mathbb E[J(t)]=0$ and $\\mathbb E[J(s)J(t)]=C(t-s)$ for all $s,t\\ge0$, with the even extension $C(-u)=C(u)$. Define $X_T:=\\int_0^T J(t)\\,dt$ as a Bochner integral in $L^2$. In this classical alternative assume $\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\int_0^R|C(t)|\\,dt<\\infty$ as an absolute improper Riemann integral. In the quantum alternative, let $V=V^*$ be the chosen spatially normalized current observable in a finite-range spin $C^*$-dynamical system with the exact hypotheses of the finite quantum variance theorem cited below, and let its invariant state $\\phi$ satisfy $\\phi(V)=0$. For its strongly continuous dynamics $\\alpha_t$, define $X_T:=\\int_0^T\\alpha_t(V)\\,dt$ as a norm-Riemann integral and $C(t):=\\operatorname{Re}\\phi(V\\alpha_t(V))$. Invariance and self-adjointness make this real correlation even. This quantum alternative assumes exactly the Countable Choice, finite-matrix $C^*$-identity, interaction, state, and dynamics hypotheses in that supplier, and requires $C\\in L^1([0,\\infty))$ with respect to Lebesgue measure. In either alternative, the corresponding integral being finite gives\n$$\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(X_T)}{2T}=\\int_0^\\infty C(t)\\,dt=:D\\ge0,$$\nwhere the classical variance is $\\mathbb E[(X_T-\\mathbb E X_T)^2]=\\mathbb E[X_T^2]$ and the quantum variance is $\\phi(X_T^2)$. The correlation already includes the chosen spatial normalization; if it is obtained from a finite-volume sequence, convergence to $C$ and the order of spatial, time, and frequency-regulator limits are assumptions, not conclusions here. If the chosen current observable $J$ or $V$ has units $\\mathsf Q\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, both sides have units $\\mathsf Q^2\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, multiplied by any declared spatial-normalization unit. This conditional variance coefficient is not by itself an empirical diffusion constant or a conductivity.",
