          "title": "Orthogonal normal form of a finite real skew matrix",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "physics",
          "statement": "For every integer $N\\ge1$ and real skew-symmetric matrix $A\\in M_{2N}(\\mathbb R)$, there are an orthogonal real matrix $O$ and numbers $\\epsilon_1,\\ldots,\\epsilon_N\\ge0$ such that\n$$OAO^{\\mathsf T}=\\bigoplus_{r=1}^N\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\epsilon_r\\\\-\\epsilon_r&0\\end{pmatrix}.$$\nFor a real square matrix $A$, $A^{\\mathsf T}A$ is positive semidefinite because $v^{\\mathsf T}A^{\\mathsf T}Av=\\sum_j(Av)_j^2\\ge0$ for every real column vector $v$. Its singular-value multiset is the multiset of nonnegative square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^{\\mathsf T}A$, each eigenvalue repeated according to its eigenspace dimension. In the displayed form, the positive $\\epsilon_r$ are precisely the positive singular values of $A$: each block with $\\epsilon_r>0$ contributes two entries equal to $\\epsilon_r$, counting repeated block values separately. Every zero block pairs two kernel directions.\n\n",
