      "items": [
        {
          "id": "ex-qsmx-ising-classical-and-field-limits",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "Classical and strong-field endpoints of the finite open chain",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "physics",
          "statement": "Let $h,J\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\nFor the finite open chain on $N\\ge1$ sites, let $\\mathcal H_N=(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes N}$ and $H_N(h,J)=-h\\sum_{j=1}^NZ_j-J\\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}X_jX_{j+1}$ as in the open-chain definition. For $s_j\\in\\{-1,1\\}$ set $x_{s_j}=2^{-1/2}(1,s_j)^{\\mathsf T}$, $z_{+1}=(1,0)^{\\mathsf T}$, and $z_{-1}=(0,1)^{\\mathsf T}$, and define the $X$- and $Z$-product basis vectors $|s\\rangle_X=\\bigotimes_{j=1}^Nx_{s_j}$ and $|s\\rangle_Z=\\bigotimes_{j=1}^Nz_{s_j}$ for $s=(s_1,\\ldots,s_N)\\in\\{-1,1\\}^N$. These are eigenbases with $X_j|s\\rangle_X=s_j|s\\rangle_X$ and $Z_j|s\\rangle_Z=s_j|s\\rangle_Z$. When $H_N$ is diagonal in one of these product bases $B\\in\\{X,Z\\}$, let $E_B(s)$ be its diagonal matrix entry at $|s\\rangle_B$; a ground configuration in that basis is a label $s$ minimizing $E_B$ over $\\{-1,1\\}^N$.\n\nWhen $h=0$ and $J>0$, the ground configurations in the $X$ product basis are exactly $s=(1,\\ldots,1)$ and $s=(-1,\\ldots,-1)$. When $J=0$ and $h\\ne0$, the unique ground configuration in the $Z$ product basis has $s_j=1$ at every site if $h>0$ and $s_j=-1$ at every site if $h<0$; thus the minimizing orientation is specified for both signs of $h$. For $\\beta>0$ in $\\mathrm{J}^{-1}$ and $h,J$ in joules, the trace is $Z_N(\\beta,h,J)=\\prod_r2\\cosh(\\beta\\epsilon_r/2)$ with the open-chain mode energies $\\epsilon_r$. For each fixed finite $N$, the function $Z_N(\\beta,h,J)=\\operatorname{Tr}_{\\mathcal H_N}(e^{-\\beta H_N(h,J)})$ is strictly positive and real-analytic on $\\mathbb R^3$; here real-analytic means that near every real triple $(\\beta,h,J)$ it is represented by a convergent real power series in those three parameters.\n\n",
