            "thm-tgr-electrochemical-stationarity",
            "def-closed-and-exact-differential-forms",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-tgrx-thermal-vorticity": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tgrx-period-of-one-form": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tgrx-exact-one-form-zero-period": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-tgr-charged-liouville-data": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tgrx-compensator-implies-exact": "mathematical-premise",
            "lem-tgr-thermal-killing-kinematic-equivalence": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-tgr-electrochemical-stationarity": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-closed-and-exact-differential-forms": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "physical_scope": null,
          "statement": "Let $O$ be a nonempty connected open subset of a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,N)$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$, with Levi-Civita connection $\\nabla$ and time-orientation field $N$. Let $\\beta$ be any smooth vector field on $O$ satisfying $g(\\beta,\\beta)<0$ and $g(\\beta,N)<0$ everywhere; it is not assumed Killing, and Killing means $\\mathcal L_\\beta g=0$. In length coordinates $x^0=ct$, take $\\beta$ to have components in $\\mathrm{J}^{-1}$, fix $k_B>0$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K}^{-1}$, and let $\\mu:O\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth in joules. Define\n$$T:=\\frac{1}{k_B\\sqrt{-g(\\beta,\\beta)}},\\qquad u:=k_BT\\beta,\\qquad \\varpi_{ab}:=-\\frac12(\\nabla_a\\beta_b-\\nabla_b\\beta_a),$$\nand\n$$h_{ab}:=g_{ab}+u_au_b,\\quad D:=u^a\\nabla_a,\\quad a_b:=u^a\\nabla_au_b,\\quad \\theta:=\\nabla_au^a,\\quad \\sigma_{ab}:=h_a{}^ch_b{}^d\\nabla_{(c}u_{d)}-\\frac13\\theta h_{ab},\\quad \\alpha:=-\\frac{\\mu}{k_BT}.$$\nThen $\\beta$ is Killing if and only if $DT=0$, $\\theta=0$, $\\sigma_{ab}=0$, and $h_a{}^b\\nabla_b\\log T=-a_a$.\n\nIn the Killing subcase, let $A$ be a global smooth real one-form with SI length-coordinate components in $\\mathrm{T\\,m}$, set $F:=dA$ (with components in teslas), and define $\\omega:=\\iota_\\beta F$ by $(\\iota_\\beta F)_a=\\beta^bF_{ba}$. The components of $\\omega$ have units $\\mathrm{T\\,J}^{-1}$. Assume $d\\omega=0$. Here exact means $\\omega=d\\psi$ for a globally defined smooth scalar $\\psi$ with units $\\mathrm{T\\,m\\,J}^{-1}$; for a piecewise-$C^1$ closed curve $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to O$, its period is the oriented integral $\\operatorname{Per}_\\gamma(\\omega):=\\sum_j\\int_{t_j}^{t_{j+1}}\\omega_{\\gamma(t)}(\\dot\\gamma(t))\\,dt$, with the curve regularity and endpoint convention in the period definition. Let $m>0$ be a particle rest mass in kilograms, let $q_{\\rm SI}\\ne0$ be a constant charge in coulombs, and take $c>0$ in metres per second. Set $q_{\\rm eff}:=cq_{\\rm SI}$, with units $\\mathrm{C\\,m\\,s}^{-1}$. Choose any constant $T_*>0$ kelvins and put $\\xi:=k_BT_*\\beta$ and $N_\\xi:=\\sqrt{-g(\\xi,\\xi)}$. Then $\\xi$ is a future-timelike Killing field with dimensionless components, $u=\\xi/N_\\xi$ is future unit timelike, $N_\\xi=T_*/T$, and $\\beta=u/(k_BT)$. These are the stationary geometric data required by the charged stationarity theorem. For the ansatz $f=\\exp(\\zeta+g(\\beta,P))$ on the future massive shell defined by the charged particle data, with $\\zeta:=\\mu/(k_BT)$ and $\\alpha:=-\\zeta$, stationarity is equivalent, in this already-Killing subcase, to $d\\alpha=q_{\\rm eff}\\omega$; both sides have inverse-length units. Thus the equation for an unknown global smooth dimensionless scalar $\\alpha$ has a solution exactly when $\\omega$ is exact; any nonzero period obstructs such a solution. The scalar $\\alpha$ corresponds to chemical potential $\\mu=-k_BT\\alpha$ in joules.\n\nIf the same normalized field $\\xi=k_BT_*\\beta$ has a global potential compensator $\\mathcal L_\\xi A=d\\Lambda_\\xi$, then $\\omega$ is exact and all its periods vanish. For the rotating model, take global Cartesian length coordinates $(x^0,x,y,z)$ on Minkowski space $M=\\mathbb R^4$, with $x^0=ct$, metric $-(dx^0)^2+dx^2+dy^2+dz^2$, and future direction selected by $\\partial_{x^0}$. Put $r:=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ and, away from the axis $r=0$, let $\\phi$ be the polar angle in the $x,y$-plane. The rotation field $\\partial_\\phi:=-y\\partial_x+x\\partial_y$ is defined by this Cartesian formula on all of $M$, including the axis; away from the axis the metric is $-(dx^0)^2+dr^2+r^2d\\phi^2+dz^2$. For $b>0$ in $\\mathrm{J}^{-1}$ and $\\Omega\\in\\mathbb R$ in $\\mathrm{m}^{-1}$, set $\\beta:=b(\\partial_{x^0}+\\Omega\\partial_\\phi)$. This field is future timelike exactly where $1-\\Omega^2r^2>0$. When $\\Omega\\ne0$ this is $r<1/|\\Omega|$ and $T=1/(k_Bb\\sqrt{1-\\Omega^2r^2})$; when $\\Omega=0$ every $r$ is allowed and $T=1/(k_Bb)$. These rotating formulas use the same SI length-coordinate convention $x^0=ct$ as the preceding clauses; no natural-unit substitution is made.\n",
