          },
          "justified_by": [],
          "forward_refs": [],
          "physical_scope": "A precisely delimited open problem for a selected four-dimensional semiclassical Einstein-scalar collapse model, an actually existing smooth solution on a stated globally hyperbolic domain, a Hadamard state, and a fixed covariant renormalization prescription. It asks for sufficient nonperturbative-in-the-scalar-amplitude conditions for finite, regulator-independent generalized-entropy differences and their future monotonicity. It asserts no solution existence, entropy convergence, regulator independence, generalized second law, or empirical result.\n",
          "statement": "Fix a fully specified four-dimensional semiclassical Einstein-scalar collapse model: its scalar field equation and couplings with SI units, fixed-coupling semiclassical equation and causal prescription, initial and boundary data, a globally hyperbolic domain, and a covariant renormalization prescription. Here causal curves are piecewise $C^1$ curves with consistently future- or past-directed causal tangent, and $J^\\pm(K)$ is the set of points reachable from or reaching a set $K$ along such curves. “Globally hyperbolic” uses the supplied Hadamard-contract convention: strong causality (each point has arbitrarily small neighborhoods met in a connected parameter interval by every causal curve) and compact causal diamonds $J^+(p)\\cap J^-(q)$. “Semiclassical” means the metric is treated as a classical field while the quantum scalar state contributes through the adopted effective equation. A covariant prescription here has the supplied local-covariance property: its assigned local data commute with pullback under smooth time-orientation-preserving isometric embeddings $\\psi:(M,g)\\to(M',g')$ with causally convex image that preserve the prescribed scalar potential, $V=\\psi^*V'$, comparing the same scale $\\mu$ and finite normalization conditions $\\mathbf c$. Potential preservation is equivalently $P(\\psi^*f)=\\psi^*(P'f)$ for every $f\\in C^\\infty(M')$. This assigns no preferred state; covariance of stress expectations is required only for supplied states whose two-point distributions satisfy $W_\\omega=(\\psi\\times\\psi)^*W_{\\omega'}$. The prescription fixes one scale $\\mu$, the local curvature counterterms, and their finite normalization conditions; the same prescription and its prescribed regulator dependence are used at both cuts and for every regulator sequence compared below. The spacetime of the selected solution is a smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g,N)$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$, with Levi-Civita connection $\\nabla$ (the unique torsion-free, metric-compatible connection); $N$ is the supplied smooth future-timelike field fixing the time orientation. Select an actually existing smooth Lorentzian metric $g$ (a smooth symmetric covariant two-tensor field on $M$) and a positive normalized Hadamard state $\\omega$ on the specified scalar-field algebra, such that $g$ and $\\omega$ satisfy the adopted semiclassical equation and the model's supplied initial and boundary data. Here $\\omega$ supplies the quantum scalar field in that equation; no additional classical scalar field $\\phi$ is part of the data unless the selected model separately specifies its equation and coupling. Its two-point distribution $W_\\omega$ is a bisolution with $\\operatorname{WF}(W_\\omega)=\\mathcal C_g^+$, where $\\mathcal C_g^+$ consists of pairs $(x,\\xi;y,-\\zeta)$ of nonzero future-null covectors that agree at a coincident point or are the metric-dual tangent covectors, with a common positive scale, along one nonconstant future-directed null geodesic. The supplied Hadamard contract also fixes a state-independent local singular bidistribution $H_\\mu$ with $W_\\omega-H_\\mu$ smooth in each specified convex normal neighborhood; such a neighborhood is an open set on which the exponential map at every point is a diffeomorphism from a star-shaped neighborhood of zero in the tangent space. No existence, uniqueness, or stability of such a solution is assumed or claimed. The model remains the adopted effective theory below its stated cutoff; “nonperturbative in the scalar field” means that the proposed sufficient conditions and argument use no truncation in the scalar-field amplitude or state correlations, not that they construct exact quantum gravity.\n\nFor the relation $\\mathcal C_g^+$ above, the two base-point parameters may occur in either order along the future-directed null geodesic; the positive-frequency designation applies to the covectors.\n\nThe separate fixed-background free-collapse scattering postulate is a Schwarzschild model for a massless minimally coupled free scalar with an incoming ground state, an exponential late-time ray map, and a supplied scattering channel. Its Schwarzschild exterior, state, ray-map, late-time convergence, and transmission assumptions are not imposed on the coupled model in this question unless the selected model independently supplies them.\n\nThe fixed-coupling equation is\n$$G_{ab}+\\Lambda_{\\mathrm{cc}}g_{ab}+\\alpha_{\\mathrm{ct}}H^{(R^2)}_{ab}+\\beta_{\\mathrm{ct}}H^{(\\operatorname{Ric}^2)}_{ab}=\\frac{8\\pi G}{c^4}T^{\\mathrm{ren}}_{ab}[g,\\omega]$$\nbelow the supplied effective cutoff. Its scalar-field causal prescription supplies $P=-g^{ab}\\nabla_a\\nabla_b+V$, with smooth real $V$ of units $\\mathrm{m^{-2}}$ in length coordinates, and a retarded map $E_R:C^\\infty_0(M)\\to C^\\infty(M)$ satisfying $PE_Rf=f=E_RPf$ and $\\operatorname{supp}(E_Rf)\\subseteq J^+(\\operatorname{supp}f)$ for every $f\\in C^\\infty_0(M)$; the initial state data are separate supplied inputs. These are adopted model premises, not a solution-existence theorem.\n\nSelect a smooth portion $\\mathcal H\\subset M$ of the future null horizon in this solution. Here $\\mathcal H$ means a smooth embedded three-dimensional submanifold for which the restriction of $g_p$ to $T_p\\mathcal H$ has one-dimensional radical\n$$L_p:=\\{v\\in T_p\\mathcal H:g_p(v,w)=0\\text{ for every }w\\in T_p\\mathcal H\\},$$\nand choose a smooth nowhere-zero future-directed field $k$ along $\\mathcal H$ with $k_p\\in L_p$ and $\\nabla_k k=0$. These are the exact smooth-null-hypersurface and affine-generator conditions of the supplied smooth null-screen definition. Its screen spaces $\\mathcal S_p:=k_p^\\perp/\\mathbb R k_p$ form a smooth rank-two bundle with the positive-definite quotient metric induced by $g$; for local screen sections represented by tangent fields $X,Y$, the screen deformation form $B([X],[Y])=g(\\nabla_Xk,Y)$ is well defined on this quotient. The expansion and shear are respectively $\\theta:=\\operatorname{tr}_{\\bar g}B$ and $\\sigma:=B-\\frac12\\theta\\bar g$; this problem imposes no stationarity, completeness, or restriction on either quantity. If $\\mathcal H$ is intended to be an event horizon, the model must additionally supply a conformal completion: $M$ is the interior of a manifold with boundary $\\overline M$, a smooth defining function $\\Omega$ is positive on $M$ and vanishes with nonzero differential at the boundary, and $\\bar g=\\Omega^2g$ extends to a smooth nondegenerate metric there. Future null infinity $\\mathscr I^+$ is the future null boundary component, where $\\bar g^{-1}(d\\Omega,d\\Omega)=0$; the portion under study is $\\partial J^-(\\mathscr I^+)\\cap M$, where $J^-(\\mathscr I^+)$ is the set of points from which a future-directed causal curve reaches $\\mathscr I^+$. The local semiclassical equation alone does not supply this global structure.\n\nUse length coordinates $x^0=ct$ and an affine parameter $\\lambda$ with units of metres; the model fixes its origin and positive normalization. On the specified common generator patch, supply a nonempty compact boundaryless smooth two-manifold $C$, an open interval $J\\subset\\mathbb R$, and a smooth embedding $\\Psi:J\\times C\\to\\mathcal H$ such that $\\partial_\\lambda\\Psi=k\\circ\\Psi$ and every map $\\Psi_\\lambda:C\\to\\mathcal H$ is an embedding with spacelike image. A cut $\\Sigma_i$ is the image of the graph of a smooth function $\\lambda_i:C\\to J$ under $\\Psi$, with positive-definite induced metric; thus it is a nonempty compact smooth spacelike two-surface and meets each generator of this patch exactly once. The cuts $\\Sigma_1$ and $\\Sigma_2$ are ordered when $\\lambda_2(x)>\\lambda_1(x)$ for every $x\\in C$. This order is preserved by every positive affine reparametrization of the generators.\n\nAt each finite ultraviolet regulator $\\Lambda_n$, whose type, units, and ultraviolet-limit convention are specified by the model, use the finite generalized-entropy data specified by the selected finite-regulator definition on each cut, with the same model, state, finite local counterterms, and fixed scheme. Each $S_{\\mathrm{gen},n}[\\Sigma_i]$ is a finite real entropy with units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. For a regulator sequence $\\mathcal R=(\\Lambda_n,\\text{finite-regulator data})$, define\n\n$$\\Delta_{\\mathcal R,n}(\\Sigma_1,\\Sigma_2):=S_{\\mathrm{gen},n}[\\Sigma_2]-S_{\\mathrm{gen},n}[\\Sigma_1].$$\n\nAn admissible sequence has $\\Lambda_n\\to+\\infty$, uses finite-regulator data from this same selected solution and state, and applies the one fixed covariant prescription at both cuts. A sufficient criterion must specify a nonempty admissible class containing at least two regulator constructions; regulator independence means that the finite limit of $\\Delta_{\\mathcal R,n}$ is identical for every sequence in that class. The target does not require the two absolute sequences $S_{\\mathrm{gen},n}[\\Sigma_i]$ to converge separately.\n\nDetermine checkable sufficient conditions on the specified model, solution, state, ordered cuts, and admissible regulator class under which, for every such pair of later cuts,\n\n$$\\Delta_{\\mathcal R,n}(\\Sigma_1,\\Sigma_2)\\longrightarrow\\Delta(\\Sigma_1,\\Sigma_2)\\in\\mathbb R,\\qquad \\Delta(\\Sigma_1,\\Sigma_2)\\ge 0,$$\n\nThe finite real value $\\Delta$ is expressed in SI entropy units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$.\n\nThe same finite $\\Delta$ must be obtained for every admissible regulator sequence. The deduction must not assume a finite modular-geometric balance postulate or any other identity equating a modular-energy change to a gravitational-entropy or area change.\n\nThe finite comparison theorem assumes nonzero finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces $H_1=H_2\\otimes H_E$, density operators $\\rho_i,\\sigma_i$ with faithful $\\sigma_i$, and $\\rho_2=\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\rho_1$, $\\sigma_2=\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\sigma_1$. For such finite density operators, $D(\\rho\\|\\sigma):=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho(\\log\\rho-\\log\\sigma))$ uses spectral logarithms and the convention $0\\log0=0$; $\\operatorname{tr}$ is the finite-dimensional trace. Set $\\delta_i:=\\operatorname{tr}((\\rho_i-\\sigma_i)(-\\log\\sigma_i))$. Its adopted balance premise is $a_2-a_1=\\delta_1-\\delta_2$, where $a_i$ are model-supplied geometric entropy data in nats, and it proves $S_{\\mathrm{gen},2}-S_{\\mathrm{gen},1}=k_B[D(\\rho_1\\|\\sigma_1)-D(\\rho_2\\|\\sigma_2)]\\ge0$. Its limit clause assumes both endpoint entropy sequences already converge finitely in one fixed scheme. This is a comparison point only; it establishes neither continuum convergence, regulator independence, semiclassical backreaction, nor the sufficient conditions requested here.\n\n",
